/* Notes for the Alaap and TrenTorch page's figures: al:<diagram>, al:arch-<id>. See figures/notes.js. */
window.FIG_NOTES = Object.assign(window.FIG_NOTES || {}, {
  "al:arch-axes": {"nodes": {"f0-mean": {"d": "f0_mean: the average fundamental frequency, scoring 0.08 on the within/between-speaker variance screen (lower is better, see the-screen node) with a gender effect size of -3.08.\n\nThis is the cleanest identity axis Alaap measures; the large gender effect size means it separates speaker groups very strongly and reliably.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 2: source and filter, what describes a voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-2"}]}, "spectral-tilt": {"d": "spectral_tilt: scores 0.19 on the screen, but correlates with f0 at r = +0.918, strong enough that the caption suggests counting the combined f0/spectral_tilt set as roughly 2.6 independent axes, not 3.\n\nIt passes the numeric screen on its own, but that number overstates how much independent information it adds once f0 is already measured."}, "vtl-cm": {"d": "vtl_cm, vocal tract length: scores 0.21, but was effectively unusable noise until its estimator was fixed at stage 17, experiment S17.\n\nThis is a case where a genuinely good identity axis was hidden by a bad measurement, not a bad axis; fixing the estimator, not replacing the axis, is what made it useful.", "links": [{"label": "Reading: formants and resonant frequency, mapped to VTL and formant measurements", "url": "https://www.phon.ox.ac.uk/source_filter_model"}]}, "hnr-db-dropped": {"d": "hnr_db, harmonics-to-noise ratio, dropped from the final axis set: scores 0.59, and fails outright on Tamil speech, at a cost of 1.32 (versus 0.3) voices for 15.6 percentage points of adherence.\n\nThis is an axis that looked plausible but was measured to actively cost more diversity than it was worth, and to do so unevenly across languages."}, "speaking-rate-1-32": {"d": "speaking_rate: scores 1.32 on the screen; three independent measurement routes agree on this number, which is itself a form of validation.\n\nA high score here, worse than lower-scoring axes, marks it as one of the properties that varies more within one speaker than it differs between speakers, not stable enough to serve as identity."}, "f0-cv-1-04": {"d": "f0_cv, the coefficient of variation of f0: scores 1.04; this measures how much a speaker's own pitch varies within an utterance, which is closer to expressiveness than to who they are.\n\nA speaker sounding more or less animated is not the same thing as a speaker sounding like a different person; this axis mixes the two."}, "jitter-0-73-shimmer-0-62": {"d": "Jitter (0.73) and shimmer (0.62): cycle-to-cycle variations in pitch and amplitude respectively, both pass the below-1.0 screen threshold and still hurt when used.\n\nThe underlying point: 'less than 1.0' is a screening threshold, not a guarantee of quality; passing the screen is necessary but not sufficient for an axis to be worth keeping.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 11: the rest of sound, frequency and how estimators lie", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-11"}]}, "snr-db": {"d": "snr_db, signal-to-noise ratio: excluded from the identity axis set entirely by design, because it measures the recording room, not the speaker.\n\nThis is a case where an easily-measured number was deliberately left out because it doesn't answer the question the taxonomy is trying to answer."}, "measured-but-unused": {"d": "A set of attributes measured but not used in the final axis set: f0_std, f0_range, duration_s, voiced_frac, f1, f2, f3, and formant_dispersion.\n\nEach was excluded either for being redundant with an axis already in use, or for not describing identity at all, a deliberate pruning, not an oversight."}, "the-screen-within-speaker-variance-over-": {"d": "The screen itself: within-speaker variance divided by between-speaker variance, computed on two takes from the same person.\n\nMuch less than 1 means an axis reliably identifies people; greater than 1 means one person varies more across takes than people differ from each other; 0.6 to 0.8 is the dangerous middle ground, it numerically passes but, as jitter and shimmer show, can still hurt in practice."}}, "edges": {}, "walk": ["the-screen-within-speaker-variance-over-", "f0-mean", "spectral-tilt", "vtl-cm", "speaking-rate-1-32", "f0-cv-1-04", "jitter-0-73-shimmer-0-62", "hnr-db-dropped", "snr-db", "measured-but-unused"]},
  "al:arch-backend": {"nodes": {"renderer-protocol": {"d": "The Renderer protocol: a single interface with mint_vector and render(vec, text, direction) methods that both backends must implement.\n\nThe caption's argument: this interface has carried a working Indic implementation since an early stage (S6) specifically because if an interface can't express your second real use case, the interface was designed wrong (ADR-003, guard 2), not because Indic support was a planned feature from day one.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}, {"label": "Stage 20: the roads not taken", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-20"}]}, "english": {"d": "The English backend, as a heading grouping the boxes below it: model, vector shape and sample rate specific to the English pipeline."}, "qwen3-tts-12hz-1-7b-base": {"d": "Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base: the English acoustic model, Apache-2.0 licensed end to end, 4.09 GB, small enough to fit a 6 GB GPU.\n\nThe permissive licence and small footprint are both load-bearing facts, not incidental; they are what make this backend servable to the public at all."}, "vector": {"d": "The English backend's speaker vector: shape (2048,).\n\nThis is large enough to exceed pgvector's HNSW index cap of 2000 dimensions, noted explicitly on the node below, which has real consequences for how identities can be stored and searched."}, "sample-rate": {"d": "The English backend's native sample rate: 24,000 Hz.\n\nThis is the same rate the training corpus itself was measured at, in the sampling and Nyquist diagram."}, "the-sole-identity-input-x-vector-only-mo": {"d": "x_vector_only_mode: a note that the 2048-dimensional vector is the sole identity input for this backend, and a specific consequence: pgvector's HNSW index caps at 2000 dimensions, so 2048 exceeds it outright.\n\nThis isn't a hypothetical limitation; it is a concrete number that rules out a specific, commonly-used index type for this backend's vectors."}, "indic": {"d": "The Indic backend, as a heading grouping the boxes below it: a different model, vector shape and sample rate from the English pipeline."}, "springlab-indic-mio-miocodec": {"d": "SPRINGLab/Indic-Mio plus MioCodec: the Indic backend's model and codec, marked dev-only because it was trained on Expresso, a CC-BY-NC-4.0 (non-commercial) licensed dataset.\n\nThe licence, not a technical limitation, is what keeps this backend out of public production, a different kind of constraint from the English backend's GPU-fit concern."}, "vector-2": {"d": "The Indic backend's speaker vector: shape (128,), sixteen times smaller than the English backend's 2048-dimensional vector.\n\nThis is one of the concrete differences the renderer protocol has to abstract over: the same interface method has to work whether the underlying vector is 2048-wide or 128-wide."}, "sample-rate-2": {"d": "The Indic backend's native sample rate: 44,100 Hz, almost double the English backend's 24,000 Hz.\n\nThis is another dimension the shared protocol abstracts away; callers of render() don't need to know which sample rate a given backend actually produces."}, "public-servable-false-enforced-in-code-i": {"d": "public_servable = false, enforced in code, not just documented: invariant I3.\n\nThe note explains why this matters: a rule that only lives in a README can be silently violated by an unrelated config change; a rule enforced in code cannot be."}}, "edges": {"renderer-protocol>english": {"d": "The renderer protocol is implemented by the English backend, one of two concrete adapters behind the shared interface."}, "renderer-protocol>english#2": {"d": "The renderer protocol is implemented by the English backend, one of two concrete adapters behind the shared interface."}, "renderer-protocol>indic": {"d": "The same protocol is implemented by the Indic backend, a structurally different model, vector width and sample rate, behind the identical interface."}, "renderer-protocol>indic#2": {"d": "The same protocol is implemented by the Indic backend, a structurally different model, vector width and sample rate, behind the identical interface."}}, "walk": ["renderer-protocol", "renderer-protocol>english", "english", "qwen3-tts-12hz-1-7b-base", "vector", "sample-rate", "the-sole-identity-input-x-vector-only-mo", "renderer-protocol>indic", "indic", "springlab-indic-mio-miocodec", "vector-2", "sample-rate-2", "public-servable-false-enforced-in-code-i"]},
  "al:arch-codec": {"nodes": {"audio-44-1-khz": {"d": "Input audio at 44.1 kHz: MioCodec's native sample rate, matching the Indic backend's sample rate from the two-backends diagram.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}]}, "encode": {"d": "encode(), called with return_content=True and return_global=True: MioCodec's single encoding call that produces both outputs this diagram is about.\n\nBoth flags have to be set explicitly; the function doesn't return everything by default.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: neural audio codecs", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/13-neural-audio-codecs"}]}, "content-tokens": {"d": "Content tokens, at 25 Hz: what is said, encoded as discrete ids from an FSQ (finite scalar quantisation) codebook.\n\nThese carry no speaker identity at all; the same content tokens, decoded with a different global embedding, would say the same words in a different voice."}, "global-embedding": {"d": "The global embedding, shape (128,): who says it, MioCodec's own identity vector, distinct from the ECAPA-TDNN speaker vector used elsewhere in the system.\n\nThis is the vector referenced as 128-dimensional in the two-backends diagram."}, "decode": {"d": "decode(), requiring both arguments to be 1-D: reconstructs a waveform from content tokens and a global embedding together.\n\nBoth inputs are required; content tokens alone or a global embedding alone are not enough to produce audio."}, "audio": {"d": "The reconstructed waveform: the final output of the content/identity split, assuming both inputs to decode() actually correspond to the same codec."}, "the-bug-that-cost-a-week": {"d": "A callout describing MioCodec's most expensive bug: two separate FSQ codecs both use the same level structure, [8,8,8,5,5], 12,800 entries, but share no actual codebook between them.\n\nBecause every index in that range is technically valid for both codecs, decode() never raises an error when tokens from one codec are fed to the other's decoder; it produces fluent, natural-sounding audio saying completely different words. A human listener evaluating only 'does this sound right?' rather than 'does this say X?' would pass it without noticing anything wrong."}}, "edges": {"audio-44-1-khz>encode": {"d": "The input waveform is passed to encode(), which produces both outputs in one call."}, "audio-44-1-khz>encode#2": {"d": "The input waveform is passed to encode(), which produces both outputs in one call."}, "encode>content-tokens": {"d": "encode() produces content tokens as one of its two outputs, when return_content=True."}, "encode>content-tokens#2": {"d": "encode() produces content tokens as one of its two outputs, when return_content=True."}, "encode>global-embedding": {"d": "encode() produces the global embedding as its other output, when return_global=True."}, "encode>global-embedding#2": {"d": "encode() produces the global embedding as its other output, when return_global=True."}, "content-tokens>decode": {"d": "Content tokens are one of decode()'s two required inputs."}, "content-tokens>decode#2": {"d": "Content tokens are one of decode()'s two required inputs."}, "global-embedding>decode": {"d": "The global embedding is decode()'s other required input, supplying identity to content tokens that carry none."}, "global-embedding>decode#2": {"d": "The global embedding is decode()'s other required input, supplying identity to content tokens that carry none."}, "decode>audio": {"d": "decode() combines both inputs into the final reconstructed waveform, correctly only if both actually came from the same codec, which is exactly what the bug callout warns can silently fail to hold."}, "decode>audio#2": {"d": "decode() combines both inputs into the final reconstructed waveform, correctly only if both actually came from the same codec, which is exactly what the bug callout warns can silently fail to hold."}}, "walk": ["audio-44-1-khz", "audio-44-1-khz>encode", "encode", "encode>content-tokens", "content-tokens", "encode>global-embedding", "global-embedding", "content-tokens>decode", "global-embedding>decode", "decode", "decode>audio", "audio", "the-bug-that-cost-a-week"]},
  "al:arch-ctx": {"nodes": {"description": {"d": "A free-text description of a character's voice, such as 'a warm, low-pitched mentor' — the only input needed to create a new identity.\n\nThis is the entry point to the whole system; everything else exists to turn this sentence into a consistent, reusable voice.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "script": {"d": "A script: up to hundreds of lines of dialogue for one character, all of which must come out sounding like the same person.\n\nIdentity is minted once from the description; the script is what gets rendered many times against that one fixed identity."}, "direction-per-line": {"d": "Per-line direction: emotion and speaking rate, specified separately for each line of dialogue.\n\nUnlike the description, which sets identity once, direction is applied fresh for every single line — the same voice can sound urgent in one line and calm in the next."}, "box": {"d": "A grouping box labelled 'alaap': the system boundary drawn around the mapper, identity store, renderer, gates and watermark — not a component itself.\n\nInputs (description, script, direction) arrow into this boundary and outputs (audio, manifest) arrow out of it; the box stands for everything Alaap does internally, abstracting away which specific inner component each input actually reaches first.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 0: orientation, what Alaap is", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-0"}, {"label": "Stage 21: capstone, Alaap from scratch", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-21"}]}, "mapper": {"d": "The mapper: converts a description into a speaker vector, the component stage 17 teaches in detail.\n\nThis is the only place in the system where free text becomes a point in speaker-vector space."}, "identity-store": {"d": "The identity store: holds a speaker vector plus a seed audio clip for every minted voice.\n\nKeeping a seed clip alongside the vector, not just the vector alone, is a deliberate redundancy, invariant I2, adopted after an encoder change once orphaned every stored vector with no way to recover them."}, "renderer": {"d": "The renderer: a frozen text-to-speech model that turns an identity plus text into speech.\n\n'Frozen' is the load-bearing word — its weights are never updated; every voice the system produces comes from feeding it a different speaker vector, not from retraining it."}, "gates": {"d": "The gates: automatic checks for drift, CER (character error rate) and uniqueness, run on rendered audio before it is accepted.\n\nThese are the identity and intelligibility gates detailed in the gate-stack diagram; nothing reaches a listener without passing them."}, "watermark": {"d": "AudioSeal watermarking, applied to every file: an inaudible signal embedded in the output so it can later be identified as AI-generated.\n\nIt runs on the very first render, not as a separate launch task — required under EU AI Act Article 50(2), invariant I7.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: anti-spoofing and audio watermarking", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/16-anti-spoofing-audio-watermarking"}]}, "audio-per-line": {"d": "The rendered audio for each line, in OGG/Vorbis format — the actual sound a listener hears.\n\nThis and the manifest are the system's only two outputs."}, "manifest-json": {"d": "manifest.json: a machine-readable summary of what was produced, structured for a game engine to consume directly.\n\nThis is what lets a game or app wire up the rendered lines without a person manually matching audio files to dialogue lines."}}, "edges": {"description>box": {"d": "The description enters the Alaap system boundary, ultimately reaching the mapper inside it."}, "description>box#2": {"d": "The description enters the Alaap system boundary, ultimately reaching the mapper inside it."}, "script>box": {"d": "The script enters the system boundary, ultimately reaching the renderer for each of its lines."}, "script>box#2": {"d": "The script enters the system boundary, ultimately reaching the renderer for each of its lines."}, "direction-per-line>box": {"d": "Per-line direction enters the system boundary, ultimately reaching the renderer alongside the script's text."}, "direction-per-line>box#2": {"d": "Per-line direction enters the system boundary, ultimately reaching the renderer alongside the script's text."}, "box>manifest-json": {"d": "The system boundary's output includes the manifest describing everything that was produced."}, "box>manifest-json#2": {"d": "The system boundary's output includes the manifest describing everything that was produced."}, "box>audio-per-line": {"d": "The system boundary's output includes the rendered audio for each line."}, "box>audio-per-line#2": {"d": "The system boundary's output includes the rendered audio for each line."}}, "walk": ["description", "script", "direction-per-line", "description>box", "script>box", "direction-per-line>box", "box", "mapper", "identity-store", "renderer", "gates", "watermark", "box>audio-per-line", "box>manifest-json", "audio-per-line", "manifest-json"]},
  "al:arch-dir": {"nodes": {"direction": {"d": "Direction: the per-line performance channel covering emotion, intensity and rate, distinct from identity, which is fixed once minted.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector": {"d": "Emotion via x + alpha*tau, applied directly to the speaker vector: a task vector (tau) fitted over 4+ speakers using the CREMA-D emotional-speech dataset.\n\nMeasured result: +0.29 on dEECS (an emotion-expression metric) with roughly zero cost to S-SIM (speaker similarity), meaning emotion can be steered without damaging how well the voice still matches its stored identity."}, "works": {"d": "A verdict label: this mechanism, vector-level emotion steering, works, based on the measured results on the node before it."}, "rate-retime-on-the-waveform": {"d": "Rate via retime(), applied to the waveform after rendering: the same phase vocoder from the render-pipeline diagram, operating over a range of 0.67x to 1.43x normal speed.\n\nThe clean usable range within those bounds is still a guess pending a dedicated experiment (S14d); the mechanism works, but its practical limits aren't fully measured yet."}, "bounded": {"d": "A verdict label: this mechanism, waveform-level rate control, is bounded; it works, but only within a range that isn't yet fully characterised."}, "emotion-tags-in-the-text": {"d": "Emotion tags inserted directly into the text, like <happy> or <whisper>, plus word-level stress marks: a third mechanism that was tried.\n\nMeasured result: 0 of 9 tested tags are actual tokens in either the deployed model or its base model; the tags have no effect because the model was never trained to recognise them as anything other than literal, meaningless text."}, "inert": {"d": "A verdict label: this mechanism, text-tag emotion control, is inert; it does nothing, because the tags aren't real tokens the model responds to."}, "invariant-i5": {"d": "Invariant I5: identity stores hold timbre only, never emotion. A voice is minted from a neutral state, then stored neutral; alpha*tau is added only at render time.\n\nThe reasoning given: baking emotion into a stored identity would make dramatic delivery impossible, since the identity would always sound however it was minted, and would break consistency across a character's several hundred lines of dialogue, since direction changes line by line but identity must not."}}, "edges": {"direction>emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector": {"d": "Direction's emotion component is realised as a task-vector addition to the speaker vector, one of three mechanisms tried."}, "direction>emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector#2": {"d": "Direction's emotion component is realised as a task-vector addition to the speaker vector, one of three mechanisms tried."}, "emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector>works": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as working, based on its measured dEECS gain at near-zero S-SIM cost."}, "emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector>works#2": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as working, based on its measured dEECS gain at near-zero S-SIM cost."}, "direction>rate-retime-on-the-waveform": {"d": "Direction's rate component is realised by the phase vocoder acting on the rendered waveform, a second mechanism."}, "direction>rate-retime-on-the-waveform#2": {"d": "Direction's rate component is realised by the phase vocoder acting on the rendered waveform, a second mechanism."}, "rate-retime-on-the-waveform>bounded": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as bounded, functional within a range that isn't yet fully characterised."}, "rate-retime-on-the-waveform>bounded#2": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as bounded, functional within a range that isn't yet fully characterised."}, "direction>emotion-tags-in-the-text": {"d": "A third mechanism tried for emotion: inserting tags directly into the text sent to the model."}, "direction>emotion-tags-in-the-text#2": {"d": "A third mechanism tried for emotion: inserting tags directly into the text sent to the model."}, "emotion-tags-in-the-text>inert": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as inert, since none of the tested tags are real tokens the model responds to."}, "emotion-tags-in-the-text>inert#2": {"d": "This mechanism is verdicted as inert, since none of the tested tags are real tokens the model responds to."}}, "walk": ["direction", "direction>emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector", "emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector", "emotion-x-alpha-tau-on-the-vector>works", "works", "direction>rate-retime-on-the-waveform", "rate-retime-on-the-waveform", "rate-retime-on-the-waveform>bounded", "bounded", "direction>emotion-tags-in-the-text", "emotion-tags-in-the-text", "emotion-tags-in-the-text>inert", "inert", "invariant-i5"]},
  "al:arch-exp": {"nodes": {"e3-scaling": {"d": "The foundations group of five experiments: E3 (scaling), E1 (navigability), E2 (vocoder drift), E4 (calibration), and E5 (watermark).\n\nThese are the experiments the caption calls the ones that changed a decision, grouped as the base the rest of the project builds on.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 18: evaluation, and why most of it is wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-18"}]}, "e0-direction-tau": {"d": "The working-system group: E0 (direction/tau), E9 (extremes hold), E10 (the 1.7B model is better), S2 (the retrieval mapper), and E11 (the English catalog).\n\nThis is the set of experiments that produced Alaap's actual working system, built in about four days according to the figcaption."}, "s4-indic-captions": {"d": "The Indic-port group: S4 (Indic captions), S5 (the MioCodec content/identity split), S6 (minting works), S7 (catalog saturates), and S8 (retrieval reaches 87%).\n\nThis is where the finding that should have redirected the project first appears: retrieval clearly outperforming minting."}, "22-effective-voices": {"d": "The wall: about 22 effective voices, against an upper bound of roughly 38, with minting reaching only 58% of that bound while retrieval reaches 87%.\n\nThe diagram marks this explicitly as THE PRODUCT ANSWER: the number that actually matters is not any individual experiment's result but this gap between what minting achieves and what the space could support."}, "box": {"d": "A grouping box: four separate attempts to close the gap between minting's 58% and retrieval's 87%, all measured, all unsuccessful.\n\nGrouping them together emphasises the pattern: four different, reasonable-sounding levers, all tried, all negative."}, "more-novelty": {"d": "More novelty, experiment S9: pushing minted vectors further from their retrieved anchors, which measurably made voices MORE central rather than more distinctive, the opposite of the intended effect."}, "more-corpus": {"d": "More corpus, experiment S7: expanding the anchor corpus from 141 to 432 speakers produced zero change in effective voice count.\n\nMore raw data did not translate into more diversity; the bottleneck was somewhere else."}, "more-bins": {"d": "More bins, experiment S18: increasing the bin grid 125x actually cost -2.7 voices rather than gaining any.\n\nA finer-grained target space made things measurably worse, not better."}, "more-axes": {"d": "More axes, experiment S18: all 17 measured attributes were audited as candidate axes, and none of them helped.\n\nThis rules out 'we're just not measuring the right thing yet' as an explanation for the plateau."}, "box-2": {"d": "A second grouping box: the pivot after all four levers failed. Instead of chasing more diversity directly, better-measure the axes already in use."}, "s17-vtl-cm-repaired": {"d": "Experiment S17: repairing the vtl_cm estimator, which changed its gender effect size from roughly 0, useless, to somewhere between +0.44 and +1.03, a real, usable identity signal.\n\nThis is the same repaired estimator referenced in the attribute-taxonomy diagram."}, "s22-axis-set-adopted": {"d": "Experiment S22: adopting a specific, finalised axis set.\n\nThe caption marks this as the one shipped change to come out of the entire measurement-improvement effort."}, "s21-s24-spectral-tilt": {"d": "Experiments S21 through S24: attempting to repair spectral_tilt, concluding it is defective and that the repair makes things worse; this lever is explicitly closed.\n\nUnlike vtl_cm, spectral_tilt's problem, its correlation with f0, wasn't something a better estimator could fix."}, "s11-s15-s19-s20-s20b-s23-s14b-s14c-natur": {"d": "The instrument track: experiments S11, S15, S19, S20, S20b, S23, S14b and S14c, the naturalness gate, listening-test sets, layer sweeps, and control-condition failures.\n\nSix of the last eight outputs from this whole line of work are caveats about what doesn't work, rather than new capabilities, a rigorous, honest, but not directly product-facing result."}}, "edges": {"e3-scaling>e0-direction-tau": {"d": "The foundational experiments feed directly into the experiments that built the working system."}, "e3-scaling>e0-direction-tau#2": {"d": "The foundational experiments feed directly into the experiments that built the working system."}, "e0-direction-tau>s4-indic-captions": {"d": "The working system's experiments lead into the Indic port's own experiment sequence."}, "e0-direction-tau>s4-indic-captions#2": {"d": "The working system's experiments lead into the Indic port's own experiment sequence."}, "s4-indic-captions>22-effective-voices": {"d": "The Indic port's experiments are what surfaced the 22-voice wall and the minting-versus-retrieval gap."}, "s4-indic-captions>22-effective-voices#2": {"d": "The Indic port's experiments are what surfaced the 22-voice wall and the minting-versus-retrieval gap."}}, "walk": ["e3-scaling", "e3-scaling>e0-direction-tau", "e0-direction-tau", "e0-direction-tau>s4-indic-captions", "s4-indic-captions", "s4-indic-captions>22-effective-voices", "22-effective-voices", "box", "more-novelty", "more-corpus", "more-bins", "more-axes", "box-2", "s17-vtl-cm-repaired", "s22-axis-set-adopted", "s21-s24-spectral-tilt", "s11-s15-s19-s20-s20b-s23-s14b-s14c-natur"]},
  "al:arch-gates": {"nodes": {"drift-0-40": {"d": "Drift gate, threshold at least 0.40: is the rendered voice the one that was actually asked for?\n\nMeasured by re-extracting a speaker embedding from the rendered probe and comparing it, by cosine similarity, to the intended vector.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 0: orientation, what Alaap is", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-0"}, {"label": "Stage 18: evaluation, and why most of it is wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-18"}]}, "consistency-0-43": {"d": "Consistency gate, threshold at least 0.43: does the voice sound like the same person across different sentences?\n\nMeasured the same way as drift, by ECAPA-TDNN cosine similarity, but between renders of different sentences from the same identity, rather than against the intended vector."}, "uniqueness-0-30": {"d": "Uniqueness gate, threshold at least 0.30, or 0.45 on MioCodec: is this a voice the system already has?\n\nThe threshold moved once, by experiment S11, evidence it is a calibrated, measured number, not a value chosen once and never revisited."}, "cer": {"d": "CER (character error rate) gate: does the rendered audio actually say the words it was asked to say, checked by comparing an ASR transcript of the render against the intended prompt.\n\nMinting itself was measured to cost about -0.012 CER relative to real recordings, a small, known intelligibility tax."}, "naturalness-lt-75": {"d": "A proposed naturalness gate, threshold below 75: distance, in WavLM's layer-7 representation, from the real-speech manifold.\n\nReal speech measures around 50 on this scale; Alaap's renders measure around 97, clearly outside the proposed threshold, but this gate has never actually rejected anything. It is a proposal, not an enforced check.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: audio evaluation metrics", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/17-audio-evaluation-metrics"}]}, "the-trap-this-avoids": {"d": "A callout, not a gate: explains why Alaap does not use an off-the-shelf naturalness predictor like DNSMOS or UTMOSv2 as a quality gate.\n\nThose predictors correlate with mean F0 at r = -0.788 and r = -0.722 respectively, while actual human judgment correlates with F0 at only r = -0.059. A MOS-based gate would systematically reject high-pitched voices for a reason no human listener actually cares about, fighting the diversity axis directly. This is invariant I9."}}, "edges": {"drift-0-40>cer": {"d": "Drift and CER are drawn adjacent in the identity-content-quality layout, though they check independent things, one identity, one intelligibility, using different measurement mechanisms with no real data dependency between them."}, "drift-0-40>cer#2": {"d": "Drift and CER are drawn adjacent in the identity-content-quality layout, though they check independent things, one identity, one intelligibility, using different measurement mechanisms with no real data dependency between them."}, "consistency-0-43>cer": {"d": "Consistency and CER are likewise drawn adjacent, though they too are independent checks with no actual data dependency."}, "consistency-0-43>cer#2": {"d": "Consistency and CER are likewise drawn adjacent, though they too are independent checks with no actual data dependency."}, "cer>naturalness-lt-75": {"d": "CER sits directly before the proposed naturalness gate in the diagram's left-to-right layout, though naturalness has never actually gated anything in practice."}, "cer>naturalness-lt-75#2": {"d": "CER sits directly before the proposed naturalness gate in the diagram's left-to-right layout, though naturalness has never actually gated anything in practice."}}, "walk": ["drift-0-40", "consistency-0-43", "uniqueness-0-30", "drift-0-40>cer", "consistency-0-43>cer", "cer", "cer>naturalness-lt-75", "naturalness-lt-75", "the-trap-this-avoids"]},
  "al:arch-geom": {"nodes": {"raw-vectors-z": {"d": "Raw speaker vectors Z: either 2048-dimensional or 128-dimensional, straight out of the encoder, with per-dimension ranges spanning three orders of magnitude.\n\nTreating this space as isotropic, as if every dimension mattered the same amount and distance meant the same thing in every direction, is exactly the mistake invariant I1 exists to prevent.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 13: is the space navigable?", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-13"}]}, "mean-centre": {"d": "Mean-centring: subtracting the average vector from every speaker vector.\n\n79.5% of any given raw vector turns out to be a shared constant offset common to all speakers, finding E3; mean-centring removes that constant so what remains actually reflects differences between speakers."}, "per-dim-rescale": {"d": "Per-dimension rescaling: normalising each dimension's scale individually, following the GVD (global variance deviation) method, with values here ranging from -6.50 to -0.14 (arXiv:2207.04834).\n\nBecause the raw dimensions span three orders of magnitude, without this step a distance computed on the raw space would be dominated by whichever dimensions happen to have the largest range, not by which dimensions actually distinguish speakers."}, "pca-basis": {"d": "The PCA basis: the actual working coordinate system after centring and rescaling.\n\nUsing the full number of dimensions was measured to work best, experiment S9b; reducing dimensionality further did not help, despite that being the more obvious move to try."}, "encode": {"d": "encode() and decode(): the two functions that move a vector between the raw encoder space and this working PCA space.\n\nEvery operation elsewhere in the system that touches a speaker vector, retrieval, blending, novelty adjustment, happens in this transformed space, not the raw one."}, "why-this-is-not-decoration": {"d": "A callout justifying why this geometry work matters in practice, not just in theory.\n\nAveraging two speaker vectors in a sparse region of the space was measured to cost +60% relative word error rate (10.94% vs 6.83%, Interspeech 2020), and descriptions like 'elderly' or 'raspy' land in exactly such sparse regions. Extrapolating past the space's outer shell was separately found 'not particularly meaningful' (ICASSP 2024). The practical rule that follows: interpolate between real anchors, stay on the shell, and never average vectors without weighting them."}}, "edges": {"raw-vectors-z>mean-centre": {"d": "Raw vectors are first mean-centred, removing the roughly 80% shared-constant component common to every speaker."}, "raw-vectors-z>mean-centre#2": {"d": "Raw vectors are first mean-centred, removing the roughly 80% shared-constant component common to every speaker."}, "mean-centre>per-dim-rescale": {"d": "The centred vectors are then rescaled per dimension, correcting for the three-orders-of-magnitude range difference between dimensions."}, "mean-centre>per-dim-rescale#2": {"d": "The centred vectors are then rescaled per dimension, correcting for the three-orders-of-magnitude range difference between dimensions."}, "per-dim-rescale>pca-basis": {"d": "The rescaled vectors define the PCA basis that becomes the actual working coordinate system."}, "per-dim-rescale>pca-basis#2": {"d": "The rescaled vectors define the PCA basis that becomes the actual working coordinate system."}, "pca-basis>encode": {"d": "Every subsequent operation on a speaker vector goes through encode()/decode() to move between the raw space and this working basis."}, "pca-basis>encode#2": {"d": "Every subsequent operation on a speaker vector goes through encode()/decode() to move between the raw space and this working basis."}}, "walk": ["raw-vectors-z", "raw-vectors-z>mean-centre", "mean-centre", "mean-centre>per-dim-rescale", "per-dim-rescale", "per-dim-rescale>pca-basis", "pca-basis", "pca-basis>encode", "encode", "why-this-is-not-decoration"]},
  "al:arch-mint": {"nodes": {"description": {"d": "Free text describing a voice — the input to the real code path in alaap/mapper.py that this whole diagram traces.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "target-bins-from-text": {"d": "target_bins_from_text(): parses the free-text description into discrete bin labels, for example 'low-pitched' or 'breathy', along the axes Alaap actually measures.\n\nThis is a simpler, separate path from the sentence encoder below it: a rule-based or lexical mapping from prose to the same bin vocabulary used elsewhere in the system, not a learned embedding."}, "textencoder": {"d": "TextEncoder: a MiniLM sentence embedding of the same description, run in parallel with the bin-label extraction.\n\nThis is the same idea as the embed diagram's tokenizer-plus-embedding-table mechanism, applied to a whole description rather than a single token."}, "retrieve": {"d": "retrieve(): a hybrid retrieval step combining the bin labels and the cosine similarity of the sentence embedding, searching the anchor corpus for the best-matching real speakers.\n\n'Hybrid' means both signals, discrete bin match and continuous embedding similarity, contribute to which anchors are retrieved, not just one or the other.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 13: is the space navigable?", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-13"}]}, "anchor-corpus": {"d": "The anchor corpus: real speakers who have actually been measured, each with a known speaker vector.\n\nRetrieval only ever returns vectors that trace back to a real, measured person; nothing here is synthesised from scratch."}, "blend-top-k-2": {"d": "Blending the top 2 retrieved anchors by SLERP (spherical linear interpolation), keeping the result at unit norm.\n\ntop_k = 2 specifically, not more: blending four anchors instead of two was measured to contract toward the centroid and lose diversity, even though it sounds like it should generalise better."}, "novelty": {"d": "A novelty parameter epsilon, defaulting to 0.0: nudges the blended vector away from its retrieved anchors by some amount.\n\nThe default is zero because pushing minted vectors away from real anchors was measured to make them more central, not more distinctive, the opposite of what 'novelty' suggests it should do."}, "speaker-vector": {"d": "The candidate speaker vector: the output of retrieval, blending and the novelty adjustment, not yet accepted as a real identity.\n\nEverything from here down is gating: deciding whether this candidate is good enough to store."}, "box": {"d": "A grouping box labelled 'gates, all must pass': the four checks, uniqueness, render-a-probe, drift, consistency and CER, a candidate vector must clear before being stored.\n\nAll of them must pass; there is no partial credit for a candidate voice.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 18: evaluation, and why most of it is wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-18"}]}, "uniqueness": {"d": "Uniqueness gate: cosine similarity below 0.30, or 0.45 on MioCodec, against every already-accepted voice — is this a voice the system already has?\n\nThe threshold itself has moved once already, by experiment S11, a sign it is a calibrated, measured number, not a value chosen once and left alone."}, "render-a-probe": {"d": "Rendering a probe: three lines of speech are actually synthesised from the frozen TTS using the candidate vector, so the remaining gates can measure the real output rather than the vector alone.\n\nThis is the step that makes the rest of the gate stack possible; drift or CER cannot be measured from a vector that was never rendered."}, "drift": {"d": "Drift gate: cosine similarity of at least 0.40 between the intended vector and the vector re-extracted from the probe render.\n\nThis is the same closed loop shown in the two-tower diagram; minting isn't accepted until the render is checked against its own origin."}, "consistency": {"d": "Consistency gate: at least 0.43 similarity across different sentences of the probe, measured by the independent ECAPA-TDNN judge.\n\nThis checks that the voice sounds like the same person across content, not just that one single render matched its intended vector."}, "cer": {"d": "CER (character error rate) gate: an ASR transcript of the probe compared against what was actually meant to be said.\n\nMinting was measured to cost about -0.012 CER relative to a real recording, a small, known intelligibility tax that comes with a synthesised, rather than real, voice."}, "identitystore-put": {"d": "IdentityStore.put(): the terminal write, storing the vector together with a seed clip, the backend id, a version number and the parameters used to produce it.\n\nOnly a candidate that has passed every gate reaches this call; nothing partially-gated gets stored."}}, "edges": {"description>target-bins-from-text": {"d": "The description is parsed directly into bin labels along the axes the system measures."}, "description>target-bins-from-text#2": {"d": "The description is parsed directly into bin labels along the axes the system measures."}, "description>textencoder": {"d": "The same description is, in parallel, embedded by the sentence encoder."}, "description>textencoder#2": {"d": "The same description is, in parallel, embedded by the sentence encoder."}, "target-bins-from-text>retrieve": {"d": "Bin labels contribute one half of retrieval's hybrid matching signal."}, "target-bins-from-text>retrieve#2": {"d": "Bin labels contribute one half of retrieval's hybrid matching signal."}, "textencoder>retrieve": {"d": "The sentence embedding contributes the other half, a continuous cosine-similarity signal."}, "textencoder>retrieve#2": {"d": "The sentence embedding contributes the other half, a continuous cosine-similarity signal."}, "anchor-corpus>retrieve": {"d": "Retrieval searches the anchor corpus of real, measured speakers for the best matches to both signals."}, "anchor-corpus>retrieve#2": {"d": "Retrieval searches the anchor corpus of real, measured speakers for the best matches to both signals."}, "retrieve>blend-top-k-2": {"d": "The top two retrieved anchors are passed forward to be blended."}, "retrieve>blend-top-k-2#2": {"d": "The top two retrieved anchors are passed forward to be blended."}, "blend-top-k-2>novelty": {"d": "The blended vector is then adjusted by the novelty parameter, by default not at all, since epsilon is 0."}, "blend-top-k-2>novelty#2": {"d": "The blended vector is then adjusted by the novelty parameter, by default not at all, since epsilon is 0."}, "novelty>speaker-vector": {"d": "The result becomes the candidate speaker vector awaiting the gates."}, "novelty>speaker-vector#2": {"d": "The result becomes the candidate speaker vector awaiting the gates."}, "speaker-vector>box": {"d": "The candidate vector enters the gate stack, where a probe is rendered from it and checked four ways."}, "speaker-vector>box#2": {"d": "The candidate vector enters the gate stack, where a probe is rendered from it and checked four ways."}, "box>identitystore-put": {"d": "A candidate that clears every gate is written to the identity store."}, "box>identitystore-put#2": {"d": "A candidate that clears every gate is written to the identity store."}}, "walk": ["description", "description>target-bins-from-text", "target-bins-from-text", "description>textencoder", "textencoder", "anchor-corpus", "target-bins-from-text>retrieve", "textencoder>retrieve", "anchor-corpus>retrieve", "retrieve", "retrieve>blend-top-k-2", "blend-top-k-2", "blend-top-k-2>novelty", "novelty", "novelty>speaker-vector", "speaker-vector", "speaker-vector>box", "box", "uniqueness", "render-a-probe", "drift", "consistency", "cer", "box>identitystore-put", "identitystore-put"]},
  "al:arch-mods": {"nodes": {"service-py": {"d": "service.py: the top-level VoiceService, exposing mint, render, and render_script as the system's public entry points.\n\nEverything else in Alaap's codebase is reached, directly or indirectly, from calls this module makes.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 21: capstone, Alaap from scratch", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-21"}]}, "mapper-py": {"d": "mapper.py: implements retrieve() and mint(), returning a MintResult, the module that turns a description into a candidate speaker vector, detailed in the mint-pipeline diagram."}, "renderer-py": {"d": "renderer.py: the backend adapters implementing the shared Renderer protocol from the two-backends diagram, for both the English and Indic pipelines."}, "identity-py": {"d": "identity.py: the IdentityStore, backed by SQLite, where accepted speaker vectors, seed clips and metadata are actually persisted."}, "watermark-py-provenance-py": {"d": "watermark.py and provenance.py, grouped as one module: AudioSeal embedding plus the hashes and parameters that make up a provenance record."}, "acoustics-py": {"d": "acoustics.py: measure(), the Binner, and BIN_LABELS, the module computing f0, formants, spectral tilt, HNR and VTL, the same measurement function used throughout the reversibility diagram."}, "geometry-py": {"d": "geometry.py: the SpeakerSpace class, mean-centring, per-dimension rescaling, PCA, and the 'shell' concept from the speaker-space-geometry diagram."}, "metrics-py": {"d": "metrics.py: Vendi score, isolation percentage, CER, naturalness, and the calibration logic behind the gate stack's thresholds."}, "encoder-py": {"d": "encoder.py: the ECAPA-TDNN wrapper, deliberately kept as the independent judge, never the model being optimised.\n\nDrift and consistency are scored by an encoder that has never seen MioCodec's output, so a good score can't just be an artefact of the generator agreeing with itself.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 12: speaker identity, embeddings that recognise people", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-12"}]}, "data-py": {"d": "data.py: stream_clips(), the CORPORA registry, a DEV_ONLY flag, and norm_gender, the module responsible for what training and measurement data is available and how it's normalised."}, "captions-py": {"d": "captions.py: PHRASES, ORDER, caption_from_bins() and its reverse, the module that turns bin labels into natural-language captions and back, the mechanism at the centre of the reversibility diagram."}, "catalog-py": {"d": "catalog.py: CATALOG_AXES (and a _V1 version), sample_cells(), and saturation logic, the module defining which attribute axes exist and how the space of possible descriptions is sampled."}, "timing-py": {"d": "timing.py: retime() and clamp_rate(), with RATE_BOUND set to 0.67-1.43, the phase-vocoder module used by both the render pipeline and the Direction channel."}, "everything-below-orchestration-is-import": {"d": "A callout, not a module: everything below the 'orchestration' row is importable and testable with no model loaded at all.\n\n135 invariant tests exercise exactly this layer, which is why the whole suite runs in about two minutes; none of it needs a GPU or a loaded checkpoint."}}, "edges": {"service-py>mapper-py": {"d": "service.py imports and calls into mapper.py, one of the four modules it composes to implement mint() and render()."}, "service-py>mapper-py#2": {"d": "service.py imports and calls into mapper.py, one of the four modules it composes to implement mint() and render()."}, "service-py>renderer-py": {"d": "service.py calls into renderer.py to actually produce audio through whichever backend is active."}, "service-py>renderer-py#2": {"d": "service.py calls into renderer.py to actually produce audio through whichever backend is active."}, "service-py>identity-py": {"d": "service.py calls into identity.py to read and write stored identities."}, "service-py>identity-py#2": {"d": "service.py calls into identity.py to read and write stored identities."}, "service-py>watermark-py-provenance-py": {"d": "service.py calls into the watermark/provenance module to embed a watermark and record provenance on every render."}, "service-py>watermark-py-provenance-py#2": {"d": "service.py calls into the watermark/provenance module to embed a watermark and record provenance on every render."}, "mapper-py>acoustics-py": {"d": "mapper.py depends on acoustics.py's measurement functions as part of its retrieval and minting logic."}, "mapper-py>acoustics-py#2": {"d": "mapper.py depends on acoustics.py's measurement functions as part of its retrieval and minting logic."}, "renderer-py>acoustics-py": {"d": "renderer.py also depends on acoustics.py, since gating a render (drift, consistency) requires measuring it first."}, "renderer-py>acoustics-py#2": {"d": "renderer.py also depends on acoustics.py, since gating a render (drift, consistency) requires measuring it first."}, "identity-py>metrics-py": {"d": "identity.py depends on metrics.py, to compute or check gate metrics before accepting a write."}, "identity-py>metrics-py#2": {"d": "identity.py depends on metrics.py, to compute or check gate metrics before accepting a write."}, "watermark-py-provenance-py>encoder-py": {"d": "The watermark/provenance module depends on encoder.py, the independent ECAPA-TDNN judge used to verify what was actually rendered."}, "watermark-py-provenance-py>encoder-py#2": {"d": "The watermark/provenance module depends on encoder.py, the independent ECAPA-TDNN judge used to verify what was actually rendered."}, "acoustics-py>data-py": {"d": "acoustics.py depends on data.py for the corpora it measures and normalises against."}, "acoustics-py>data-py#2": {"d": "acoustics.py depends on data.py for the corpora it measures and normalises against."}, "geometry-py>captions-py": {"d": "geometry.py depends on captions.py; the working PCA space feeds directly into how bin labels are turned into captions."}, "geometry-py>captions-py#2": {"d": "geometry.py depends on captions.py; the working PCA space feeds directly into how bin labels are turned into captions."}, "metrics-py>catalog-py": {"d": "metrics.py depends on catalog.py's axis and cell definitions to compute isolation and saturation metrics."}, "metrics-py>catalog-py#2": {"d": "metrics.py depends on catalog.py's axis and cell definitions to compute isolation and saturation metrics."}, "renderer-py>timing-py": {"d": "renderer.py depends on timing.py for the phase-vocoder retime() call used in the render pipeline."}, "renderer-py>timing-py#2": {"d": "renderer.py depends on timing.py for the phase-vocoder retime() call used in the render pipeline."}}, "walk": ["service-py", "service-py>mapper-py", "mapper-py", "service-py>renderer-py", "renderer-py", "service-py>identity-py", "identity-py", "service-py>watermark-py-provenance-py", "watermark-py-provenance-py", "mapper-py>acoustics-py", "renderer-py>acoustics-py", "acoustics-py", "identity-py>metrics-py", "metrics-py", "watermark-py-provenance-py>encoder-py", "encoder-py", "acoustics-py>data-py", "data-py", "geometry-py", "geometry-py>captions-py", "captions-py", "metrics-py>catalog-py", "catalog-py", "renderer-py>timing-py", "timing-py", "everything-below-orchestration-is-import"]},
  "al:arch-render": {"nodes": {"identity-id": {"d": "identity_id: a reference to an already-stored, immutable identity — nothing about the stored vector changes at render time.\n\nThis immutability is invariant I5: identity is fixed once minted, and performance (this diagram) never writes back into it.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 0: orientation, what Alaap is", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-0"}, {"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}]}, "line-text": {"d": "The text of one line of dialogue: the content half of what gets rendered, independent of which identity is speaking it."}, "direction": {"d": "Direction: emotion (alpha, tau) and speaking rate in the range 0.67 to 1.43x normal, per-line performance instructions, distinct from identity.\n\nThis is the mechanism detailed in the Direction-channel diagram: some of it, emotion, works on the vector itself; some, rate, works on the waveform afterward."}, "frozen-tts": {"d": "The frozen TTS model: combines the stored identity, the line's text, and direction into raw audio, the same frozen renderer as the system-context and two-tower diagrams.\n\nIts weights never change; direction and identity are both just different inputs to the same fixed function."}, "retime": {"d": "retime(): a phase vocoder that stretches or compresses the waveform's duration without changing its pitch, applying the rate portion of direction.\n\nThis runs after the frozen TTS produces audio; rate is a waveform-level edit, not something the frozen model is asked to do internally."}, "audioseal": {"d": "AudioSeal: embeds an inaudible watermark into the retimed audio.\n\nThis runs on every single render, including the very first one, not as a separate step added before shipping.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: anti-spoofing and audio watermarking", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/16-anti-spoofing-audio-watermarking"}]}, "line-audio": {"d": "The final per-line audio file, in OGG/Vorbis format: what a listener actually hears for this one line."}, "provenance-record": {"d": "A provenance record: a hash of the output, the parameters used to produce it, and which backend rendered it.\n\nThis is what lets a specific rendered file be traced back to exactly how it was produced, independent of the watermark itself."}}, "edges": {"identity-id>frozen-tts": {"d": "The stored identity is one of three inputs the frozen renderer combines for this line."}, "identity-id>frozen-tts#2": {"d": "The stored identity is one of three inputs the frozen renderer combines for this line."}, "line-text>frozen-tts": {"d": "The line's text is the content input to the same renderer call."}, "line-text>frozen-tts#2": {"d": "The line's text is the content input to the same renderer call."}, "direction>frozen-tts": {"d": "Direction, at least its emotion component, is applied at this same rendering step, alongside identity and text."}, "direction>frozen-tts#2": {"d": "Direction, at least its emotion component, is applied at this same rendering step, alongside identity and text."}, "frozen-tts>retime": {"d": "The renderer's raw output is passed to the phase vocoder, which applies direction's rate component afterward."}, "frozen-tts>retime#2": {"d": "The renderer's raw output is passed to the phase vocoder, which applies direction's rate component afterward."}, "retime>audioseal": {"d": "The retimed audio is watermarked before it becomes a final file."}, "retime>audioseal#2": {"d": "The retimed audio is watermarked before it becomes a final file."}, "audioseal>line-audio": {"d": "The watermarked audio becomes the final per-line audio file."}, "audioseal>line-audio#2": {"d": "The watermarked audio becomes the final per-line audio file."}, "audioseal>provenance-record": {"d": "The same watermarking step also produces the provenance record for this render."}, "audioseal>provenance-record#2": {"d": "The same watermarking step also produces the provenance record for this render."}}, "walk": ["identity-id", "line-text", "direction", "identity-id>frozen-tts", "line-text>frozen-tts", "direction>frozen-tts", "frozen-tts", "frozen-tts>retime", "retime", "retime>audioseal", "audioseal", "audioseal>line-audio", "line-audio", "audioseal>provenance-record", "provenance-record"]},
  "al:arch-rev": {"nodes": {"real-audio": {"d": "A real recording of a voice: the starting point for building a caption dataset the reversible way.\n\nMost approaches ask a model to describe a voice directly from audio, a claim that then cannot be independently checked."}, "measure": {"d": "measure(): extracts 17 numeric acoustic attributes from the audio, f0, formants, spectral tilt, HNR, VTL and more, the same estimator that later re-measures generated audio too.\n\nBecause it's the same code path both times, any claim about a caption can be checked by re-running this exact function on the render.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 11: the rest of sound, frequency and how estimators lie", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-11"}]}, "binner": {"d": "The Binner: converts each continuous numeric attribute into one of a small number of percentile-based labels, roughly five per axis.\n\nThis is what turns a raw number like f0_mean = 180 Hz into something expressible in words, like 'low-pitched.'"}, "caption-from-bins": {"d": "caption_from_bins(): turns the discrete bin labels into a natural-language caption, such as 'low-pitched, bright, ...'\n\nBecause the caption is generated from measured bins rather than written by a human listening to the audio, it is exactly recoverable from the measurements; nothing about it depends on subjective description."}, "mapper-fit-then-mint": {"d": "Fitting the mapper on caption-and-vector pairs, then minting: the mapper from the mdn diagram is trained on exactly this reversible data, then used to mint a new voice from a new description.\n\nThis is the same mint pipeline detailed elsewhere, here shown as the training-data consumer rather than the inference-time mapper."}, "generated-audio": {"d": "Audio generated from the minted identity: the render whose caption is being checked."}, "measure-again": {"d": "Running measure() again, the identical code path used on the real audio at the start.\n\nUsing the same function both times, rather than a different or human-driven process, is what makes the two measurements directly comparable."}, "data-speech-computes-9-columns-from-5-to": {"d": "A note comparing Alaap's measurement pipeline to Data-Speech, an existing captioning tool: Data-Speech computes 9 columns from 5 external tools, while Alaap measures 17 attributes with its own code.\n\nSix of Alaap's attributes, jitter, shimmer, HNR, spectral tilt, formants and VTL, are not among Data-Speech's 9 and had to be built from scratch for this project."}}, "edges": {"real-audio>measure": {"d": "The real recording is run through the same measurement function used everywhere else in this loop."}, "real-audio>measure#2": {"d": "The real recording is run through the same measurement function used everywhere else in this loop."}, "measure>binner": {"d": "The 17 numeric measurements are converted into percentile-based bin labels."}, "measure>binner#2": {"d": "The 17 numeric measurements are converted into percentile-based bin labels."}, "binner>caption-from-bins": {"d": "The bin labels are turned into a natural-language caption."}, "binner>caption-from-bins#2": {"d": "The bin labels are turned into a natural-language caption."}, "caption-from-bins>mapper-fit-then-mint": {"d": "Caption-and-vector pairs like this one are the training data the mapper is fit on, before it mints a new identity from a new description."}, "caption-from-bins>mapper-fit-then-mint#2": {"d": "Caption-and-vector pairs like this one are the training data the mapper is fit on, before it mints a new identity from a new description."}, "mapper-fit-then-mint>generated-audio": {"d": "The minted identity is rendered into audio."}, "mapper-fit-then-mint>generated-audio#2": {"d": "The minted identity is rendered into audio."}, "generated-audio>measure-again": {"d": "The generated audio is measured by the identical measure() function used on real audio at the start."}, "generated-audio>measure-again#2": {"d": "The generated audio is measured by the identical measure() function used on real audio at the start."}, "measure-again>measure": {"d": "This loop-back arrow is the point of the whole diagram: the same measurement code closes the loop, so any claim in the original caption can be checked by re-measuring the render."}, "measure-again>measure#2": {"d": "This loop-back arrow is the point of the whole diagram: the same measurement code closes the loop, so any claim in the original caption can be checked by re-measuring the render."}}, "walk": ["real-audio", "real-audio>measure", "measure", "measure>binner", "binner", "binner>caption-from-bins", "caption-from-bins", "caption-from-bins>mapper-fit-then-mint", "mapper-fit-then-mint", "mapper-fit-then-mint>generated-audio", "generated-audio", "generated-audio>measure-again", "measure-again", "measure-again>measure", "data-speech-computes-9-columns-from-5-to"]},
  "al:arch-stages": {"nodes": {"s0-foundations": {"d": "Track A (ML), stage S0, foundations: 7 of 10 planned experiments run, with E6, E7 and E8 still outstanding.\n\nThis is genuinely partial, not complete; the status board is honest about exactly which experiments are missing, not just an overall percentage.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 19: making it run, inference measured", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-19"}, {"label": "Stage 21: capstone, Alaap from scratch", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-21"}]}, "s1-identity": {"d": "Track A, S1, identity: done and verified.\n\nThis is the mint-and-gate pipeline detailed across several of the other architecture diagrams."}, "s2-retrieval": {"d": "Track A, S2, retrieval: done.\n\nThis is the retrieval mechanism that reaches 87% of the diversity bound, versus minting's 58%, per the experiment-lineage diagram."}, "s3-generative": {"d": "Track A, S3, generative: not started."}, "s4-data-v2": {"d": "Track A, S4, data v2: partial."}, "s5-indic": {"d": "Track A, S5, Indic: passed."}, "s6-inference-service": {"d": "Track B (platform), S6, inference service: mint and render available over HTTP; not started.\n\nThis is the same service the serve diagram, profile, quantise, prune, benchmark, describes building."}, "s7-gpu-hosting": {"d": "Track B, S7, GPU hosting: measuring p95 latency and cost per minute; not started."}, "s8-web-app": {"d": "Track B, S8, web app: a stranger can mint a voice unaided; not started."}, "s9-multi-tenant": {"d": "Track B, S9, multi-tenant: not started."}, "s10-release": {"d": "Track B, S10, release: not started."}, "minimum-shippable-thing-s0-s1-s6-s7-cut-": {"d": "A grouping box defining the minimum shippable thing as S0 + S1 + S6 + S7 + a cut-down S8, a deliberately smaller subset of the full ten-stage plan.\n\nIt quotes the plan's own warning: research on S2 and S3 is the most likely failure mode, because it can swallow the calendar while nothing actually ships. This box is a scope-protection device, not a status report."}, "s0": {"d": "A status chip inside the minimum-shippable-thing box: S0, done, restating s0-foundations' status in the smaller, ship-focused subset."}, "s1": {"d": "A status chip: S1, done, restating s1-identity's status."}, "s6": {"d": "A status chip: S6, not done, restating s6-inference-service's status."}, "s7": {"d": "A status chip: S7, not done, restating s7-gpu-hosting's status."}, "s8": {"d": "A status chip: S8, not done, restating s8-web-app's status, though the minimum shippable thing only needs a cut-down version of it, not the full S8."}}, "edges": {"s0-foundations>s1-identity": {"d": "Track A's stages run in sequence; identity work builds on the foundational experiments being far enough along."}, "s0-foundations>s1-identity#2": {"d": "Track A's stages run in sequence; identity work builds on the foundational experiments being far enough along."}, "s1-identity>s2-retrieval": {"d": "Retrieval work follows identity, both in the plan's ordering and in practice, since retrieval draws on the same anchor corpus and vector space."}, "s1-identity>s2-retrieval#2": {"d": "Retrieval work follows identity, both in the plan's ordering and in practice, since retrieval draws on the same anchor corpus and vector space."}, "s2-retrieval>s3-generative": {"d": "Generative work is the next planned stage after retrieval, and remains not started."}, "s2-retrieval>s3-generative#2": {"d": "Generative work is the next planned stage after retrieval, and remains not started."}, "s3-generative>s4-data-v2": {"d": "Data v2 follows generative work in the plan's stated order."}, "s3-generative>s4-data-v2#2": {"d": "Data v2 follows generative work in the plan's stated order."}, "s4-data-v2>s5-indic": {"d": "The Indic port follows data v2 in track A's sequence."}, "s4-data-v2>s5-indic#2": {"d": "The Indic port follows data v2 in track A's sequence."}, "s6-inference-service>s7-gpu-hosting": {"d": "Track B's stages also run in sequence: GPU hosting depends on the inference service existing first."}, "s6-inference-service>s7-gpu-hosting#2": {"d": "Track B's stages also run in sequence: GPU hosting depends on the inference service existing first."}, "s7-gpu-hosting>s8-web-app": {"d": "The web app stage follows GPU hosting."}, "s7-gpu-hosting>s8-web-app#2": {"d": "The web app stage follows GPU hosting."}, "s8-web-app>s9-multi-tenant": {"d": "Multi-tenant support follows the single-tenant web app."}, "s8-web-app>s9-multi-tenant#2": {"d": "Multi-tenant support follows the single-tenant web app."}, "s9-multi-tenant>s10-release": {"d": "Release is the final planned stage, after multi-tenancy."}, "s9-multi-tenant>s10-release#2": {"d": "Release is the final planned stage, after multi-tenancy."}}, "walk": ["s0-foundations", "s0-foundations>s1-identity", "s1-identity", "s1-identity>s2-retrieval", "s2-retrieval", "s2-retrieval>s3-generative", "s3-generative", "s3-generative>s4-data-v2", "s4-data-v2", "s4-data-v2>s5-indic", "s5-indic", "s6-inference-service", "s6-inference-service>s7-gpu-hosting", "s7-gpu-hosting", "s7-gpu-hosting>s8-web-app", "s8-web-app", "s8-web-app>s9-multi-tenant", "s9-multi-tenant", "s9-multi-tenant>s10-release", "s10-release", "minimum-shippable-thing-s0-s1-s6-s7-cut-", "s0", "s1", "s6", "s7", "s8"]},
  "al:arch-tower": {"nodes": {"description-text": {"d": "The free-text voice description, the same input as the system-context diagram, here viewed as the start of 'tower A.'\n\nTower A is the learned half of the system: everything from here to the speaker vector is trained.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 0: orientation, what Alaap is", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-0"}, {"label": "Stage 13: is the space navigable?", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-13"}, {"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}]}, "text-encoder": {"d": "A sentence-transformer: encodes the description into a fixed embedding, the same mechanism as the mdn diagram's text encoder.\n\nThis is a pretrained model, not trained from scratch for Alaap — only the mapper head after it is."}, "mapper-head": {"d": "The mapper head: one of three interchangeable mechanisms, retrieval, MDN, or a normalising flow, that turns a text embedding into a speaker vector.\n\nThis is the only genuinely learned, Alaap-specific component in the whole two-tower design — everything else is either a pretrained encoder or a frozen renderer.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "speaker-vector": {"d": "The speaker vector: either 2048-dimensional (the English backend's encoder) or 128-dimensional (the Indic/MioCodec backend) — the single channel connecting the two towers.\n\nThe caption calls this out explicitly: it is the ONLY channel between tower A and tower B. Nothing else about the description crosses over."}, "box": {"d": "A grouping box labelled 'tower B, frozen': the boundary around the acoustic language model, codec decoder and audio output.\n\nEverything inside this box is pretrained and never updated by Alaap's own training; only the speaker vector coming in from tower A changes what it produces."}, "text-to-speak": {"d": "The literal words to be spoken, separate from the description that produced the speaker vector.\n\nThis is content, not identity; it is what makes the two-tower split possible, since the acoustic LM needs both text and a voice, from two entirely separate sources."}, "acoustic-lm-weights-never-updated": {"d": "The acoustic language model, Qwen3-TTS 1.7B for English or Indic-Mio for the Indic backend, with weights never updated by Alaap.\n\nThis is 'tower B, frozen': it already knows how to turn text and a speaker vector into codec tokens; Alaap only ever supplies it a new vector, never retrains it.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: audio language models", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/10-audio-language-models"}]}, "codec-decoder": {"d": "The codec decoder: turns the acoustic LM's predicted tokens, plus the speaker identity, into a waveform, the same mechanism as the ttslm diagram.\n\nThis is the final step inside the frozen tower."}, "audio": {"d": "The rendered audio: tower B's output, and also the input to the drift-check loop that feeds back to compare against the original description."}}, "edges": {"description-text>text-encoder": {"d": "The description text is encoded by the sentence-transformer into a fixed embedding."}, "description-text>text-encoder#2": {"d": "The description text is encoded by the sentence-transformer into a fixed embedding."}, "text-encoder>mapper-head": {"d": "The text embedding is passed to the mapper head, whichever mechanism, retrieval, MDN, or flow, is in use."}, "text-encoder>mapper-head#2": {"d": "The text embedding is passed to the mapper head, whichever mechanism, retrieval, MDN, or flow, is in use."}, "mapper-head>speaker-vector": {"d": "The mapper head outputs a speaker vector, the single point of contact between the two towers."}, "mapper-head>speaker-vector#2": {"d": "The mapper head outputs a speaker vector, the single point of contact between the two towers."}, "box>description-text": {"d": "The dashed feedback path: audio rendered from tower B is re-encoded and compared back against the original description, closing the loop the drift gate checks (Panariello et al., Interspeech 2023). This is a verification loop run after rendering, not a forward data path."}, "box>description-text#2": {"d": "The dashed feedback path: audio rendered from tower B is re-encoded and compared back against the original description, closing the loop the drift gate checks (Panariello et al., Interspeech 2023). This is a verification loop run after rendering, not a forward data path."}}, "walk": ["description-text", "description-text>text-encoder", "text-encoder", "text-encoder>mapper-head", "mapper-head", "mapper-head>speaker-vector", "speaker-vector", "box", "text-to-speak", "acoustic-lm-weights-never-updated", "codec-decoder", "audio", "box>description-text"]},
  "al:attention": {"nodes": {"x": {"d": "The input sequence: shape (n, d) — n tokens, each represented by a d-dimensional vector already carrying embedding and position information.\n\nAttention transforms this into a new (n, d) representation where each token's output can depend on every other token's input.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 15: attention and transformers", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-15"}, {"label": "Session: embeddings and attention, at mechanism level", "url": "index.html#/s/4/3"}]}, "q": {"d": "Q = x @ Wq: the query — what each token is 'looking for', obtained by a learned linear projection of the input.\n\nEvery one of the n tokens gets its own query vector."}, "k": {"d": "K = x @ Wk: the key — what each token 'offers' to be matched against, another learned linear projection of the same input.\n\nA token's key is compared against every other token's query to determine how much attention flows between them."}, "v": {"d": "V = x @ Wv: the value — what each token actually passes on once attention decides to look at it, a third learned linear projection.\n\nQuery and key only decide how much attention to pay; value is the content that attention actually mixes forward."}, "s": {"d": "Q K^T / sqrt(d_k): the raw attention scores, an (n, n) matrix — this is where attention's quadratic memory cost comes from, since every one of n tokens scores every other one.\n\nDividing by sqrt(d_k) keeps the scores from growing too large as the dimension d_k increases, which matters for the next step.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: attention", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/12_attention"}, {"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: self-attention from scratch", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/07-transformers-deep-dive/02-self-attention-from-scratch"}]}, "sm": {"d": "Masking, then softmax: an optional mask blocks disallowed connections (for instance, future tokens in a causal model), then softmax normalises each row of the score matrix so its weights sum to 1.\n\nWithout the sqrt(d_k) scaling from the previous step, large dot products would push softmax into its flat, saturated tails, where gradients vanish — the same saturation problem as the loss diagram's sigmoid+MSE case."}, "o": {"d": "weights @ V: each token's output is a weighted combination of every token's value, using the normalised attention weights.\n\nThis produces one (n, d) output per attention head; a full transformer layer runs several heads like this in parallel and concatenates their outputs.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: multi-head attention", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/07-transformers-deep-dive/03-multi-head-attention"}]}}, "edges": {"x>q": {"d": "The input tokens are projected into queries through a learned weight matrix."}, "x>k": {"d": "The same input tokens are projected into keys through a separate learned weight matrix."}, "x>v": {"d": "The same input tokens are projected a third way, into values, through a third learned weight matrix."}, "q>s": {"d": "Queries are combined with keys by dot product to produce the raw attention scores."}, "k>s": {"d": "Keys are the other half of the dot product that produces the raw attention scores between every pair of tokens."}, "s>sm": {"d": "The raw scores are masked, where applicable, and normalised by softmax into per-row attention weights."}, "sm>o": {"d": "The normalised weights determine how much of each token's value contributes to the output."}, "v>o": {"d": "Values are the actual content mixed forward, weighted by the attention weights computed from queries and keys."}}, "walk": ["x", "x>q", "x>k", "x>v", "q", "k", "q>s", "k>s", "s", "s>sm", "sm", "v", "sm>o", "v>o", "o"]},
  "al:autograd": {"nodes": {"x": {"d": "x and W, both marked requires_grad: tensors that autograd will track, recording every operation applied to them.\n\nOnly tensors with this flag set accumulate a .grad; everything else is treated as a constant during backpropagation.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: autograd", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/06_autograd"}, {"label": "Stage 7: autograd, the gradient engine", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-7"}]}, "mm": {"d": "MatmulBackward: the backward-pass node autograd creates for a matrix multiplication, saving x and W from the forward pass because computing the gradient needs them.\n\nEvery differentiable operation saves whatever its own backward rule requires — this is what the 'tape' actually stores, not just the operation's name."}, "relu": {"d": "ReluBackward: the backward node for ReLU, saving a boolean mask of which inputs were positive.\n\nReLU's gradient is 1 where the input was positive and 0 otherwise, so the mask is all it needs to remember; it doesn't need to save the actual input values."}, "L": {"d": "The scalar loss, on which .backward() is called to start backpropagation.\n\nCalling backward() on anything other than a scalar requires an explicit gradient argument, because 'the gradient of a vector with respect to itself' isn't a single well-defined direction."}, "g3": {"d": "The seed gradient: backward() seeds the loss's own gradient as 1 (dL/dL = 1), the starting value the chain rule multiplies through.\n\nEvery other gradient in the graph is this value multiplied by a chain of local derivatives, walking the tape backward from here."}, "g2": {"d": "The gradient after ReLU's backward step: the incoming gradient multiplied elementwise by ReLU's saved mask.\n\nThis zeroes the gradient anywhere the forward pass had a negative (clipped) input — exactly mirroring what ReLU did on the way forward."}, "g1": {"d": "The final parameter gradients: x.grad = g @ W.T and W.grad = x.T @ g, one flowing back to each input of the original matmul.\n\nEach uses the forward tensor MatmulBackward saved for the other input, transposed — which is why that backward node needed to save both x and W in the first place. The diagram's own label lists the two gradients and formulas in swapped order; see issues."}}, "edges": {"x>mm": {"d": "x and W are the inputs to the matmul, both tracked by autograd since they require gradients."}, "mm>relu": {"d": "The matmul's output feeds into ReLU on the forward pass; on the tape, MatmulBackward precedes ReluBackward."}, "relu>L": {"d": "ReLU's output eventually contributes to the scalar loss."}, "L>g3": {"d": "Calling loss.backward() seeds the gradient of the loss with respect to itself as 1."}, "g3>g2": {"d": "The seed gradient is multiplied by ReLU's saved mask, zeroing it wherever the forward pass clipped a negative value."}, "g2>g1": {"d": "The gradient after ReLU is passed into MatmulBackward, which uses it together with the saved x and W to produce the two gradients below (x.grad and W.grad)."}}, "walk": ["x", "x>mm", "mm", "mm>relu", "relu", "relu>L", "L", "L>g3", "g3", "g3>g2", "g2", "g2>g1", "g1"]},
  "al:conv1d": {"nodes": {"mel": {"d": "80 mel bands: a mel-spectrogram representation of audio, one column of 80 frequency-band energies for every 10 milliseconds.\n\nThis is the standard input representation for speech models — it compresses raw waveform samples into a time-by-frequency grid before any neural network sees it.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: spectrograms and mel features", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/02-spectrograms-mel-features"}]}, "c1": {"d": "A 1-D convolution over time: a kernel of size 5 slides along the time axis, and the same small filter is applied at every position.\n\nBecause the filter is shared across all time positions, the number of parameters doesn't grow with the length of the utterance — the same mechanism as a 2-D convolution over an image, but along one axis (time) instead of two.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: convolutions", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/09_convolutions"}, {"label": "Stage 10: convolutions, and batch normalisation", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-10"}]}, "c2": {"d": "A dilated 1-D convolution: the kernel's taps are spread further apart in time, so it sees a wider window of context for the same number of parameters and the same computational cost as c1.\n\nStacking dilated convolutions is how ECAPA-TDNN builds up a large receptive field cheaply, rather than using a much larger kernel."}, "bn": {"d": "BatchNorm: normalises each channel's activations to zero mean and unit variance across the batch, then rescales with a learned scale and shift.\n\nIt stabilises training by keeping activation statistics consistent as the network's weights change underneath it, applied independently per channel."}, "pool": {"d": "Attentive statistics pooling: a weighted mean and standard deviation over the time axis, where the weights are themselves learned rather than uniform.\n\nThis is what collapses a variable-length sequence of frames into one fixed-length summary — the network learns which time steps matter most for identity rather than averaging all of them equally."}, "emb": {"d": "The fixed-length speaker embedding: whatever the length of the input utterance, pooling produces one vector of the same size.\n\nThis is what makes ECAPA-TDNN usable for speaker verification — comparing two speakers only requires comparing two fixed-size vectors, regardless of how long each recording was."}}, "edges": {"mel>c1": {"d": "The mel-spectrogram is the input the first convolution slides its kernel across."}, "c1>c2": {"d": "The first convolution's output feeds into a dilated convolution, widening the effective receptive field."}, "c2>bn": {"d": "The dilated convolution's output is normalised per channel by BatchNorm before going deeper."}, "c2>pool": {"d": "The dilated convolution's output, across the whole time axis, is also fed into attentive pooling to summarise the utterance."}, "pool>emb": {"d": "Pooling collapses the variable-length sequence into the final fixed-length speaker embedding."}}, "walk": ["mel", "mel>c1", "c1", "c1>c2", "c2", "c2>bn", "bn", "c2>pool", "pool", "pool>emb", "emb"]},
  "al:data": {"nodes": {"ds": {"d": "A Dataset: an object implementing __len__ (how many samples exist) and __getitem__ (fetch sample i) — the minimal interface a DataLoader needs.\n\nIt does not have to hold all its data in memory; __getitem__ can read a single file from disk on demand.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: dataloader", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/05_dataloader"}, {"label": "Stage 6: the data pipeline", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-6"}]}, "sh": {"d": "Shuffling: generating a random permutation of the dataset's indices (0..N-1), not touching the underlying data.\n\nEach epoch gets a new permutation, so batches are drawn from the dataset in a different order every time, without ever copying or reordering the samples themselves."}, "bt": {"d": "Taking a batch: slicing batch_size indices off the shuffled permutation and fetching just those samples via __getitem__.\n\nOnly this many samples are loaded into memory at once — the rest of the dataset stays wherever it was (disk, a database, or elsewhere)."}, "col": {"d": "Collate: stacking the batch's individual samples into one tensor with a leading batch dimension.\n\nThis is the last step before a batch is ready to feed into a model; it turns a list of separate samples into a single (batch_size, ...) tensor."}, "mem": {"d": "Memory per batch: batch_size multiplied by the size in bytes of one sample.\n\nThis is the number that actually matters for whether training fits in memory — not the size of the whole dataset, since only one batch is ever materialised as a tensor at a time."}}, "edges": {"ds>sh": {"d": "The dataset's length determines the range of indices that get shuffled into a permutation."}, "sh>bt": {"d": "A batch is taken as the next batch_size indices off the shuffled permutation."}, "bt>col": {"d": "The samples fetched for this batch are stacked together into one tensor."}, "bt>mem": {"d": "The size of one batch — not the whole dataset — is what determines memory use per step."}}, "walk": ["ds", "ds>sh", "sh", "sh>bt", "bt", "bt>col", "col", "bt>mem", "mem"]},
  "al:embed": {"nodes": {"txt": {"d": "\"warm voice\": a piece of free text, the input before any numeric processing begins.\n\nEvery step that follows exists to turn this string into numbers a neural network can operate on.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 14: tokens and embeddings", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-14"}]}, "tok": {"d": "A BPE (byte-pair encoding) tokenizer: splits text into subword pieces and maps each to an integer id — here, ids [812, 4431].\n\nBPE handles words it has never seen by falling back to smaller, known subword pieces, which is why it doesn't need a fixed dictionary of whole words.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: subword tokenization", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/05-nlp-foundations-to-advanced/19-subword-tokenization"}]}, "tab": {"d": "The embedding table: a weight matrix of shape (vocab, d) — one learned row per possible token id.\n\nAn embedding is just a row of this matrix; gradient descent is what wrote the numbers in it, the same way it writes any other learned parameter.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: embeddings", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/11_embeddings"}]}, "pos": {"d": "Positional information, learned or sinusoidal: added to each token's embedding so the network can tell position 1 from position 5, since the embedding lookup alone has no notion of order.\n\nWithout this, 'warm voice' and 'voice warm' would produce the identical set of embeddings in a different order, which a permutation-invariant operation like attention couldn't distinguish.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: positional encoding", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/07-transformers-deep-dive/04-positional-encoding"}]}, "vec": {"d": "The resulting (tokens, d) tensor: one row per input token, each row now carrying both its identity (from the embedding table) and its position.\n\nThis is the actual input to a transformer's first layer — never the raw token ids."}, "audio": {"d": "Audio, represented via a codec, at roughly 25 Hz of content tokens: the same idea as text tokenization, applied to sound.\n\nA neural audio codec turns a continuous waveform into a sequence of discrete integer ids, the same way a tokenizer turns text into ids — which is precisely what lets a language model generate audio the same way it generates text.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: neural audio codecs", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/13-neural-audio-codecs"}]}}, "edges": {"txt>tok": {"d": "The raw text string is split into subword pieces and mapped to token ids by the tokenizer."}, "tok>tab": {"d": "Each token id is used as a row index into the embedding table — that row is the token's learned vector."}, "tab>vec": {"d": "The looked-up rows for every token in the sequence form the (tokens, d) embedding tensor."}, "pos>vec": {"d": "A positional encoding is added elementwise to the token embeddings, injecting order information the lookup itself doesn't carry."}, "audio>tok": {"d": "An audio codec plays the same conceptual role for sound that the BPE tokenizer plays for text — turning a continuous signal into discrete ids that can be embedded the same way."}}, "walk": ["txt", "txt>tok", "tok", "tok>tab", "tab", "tab>vec", "pos", "pos>vec", "vec", "audio", "audio>tok"]},
  "al:layer": {"nodes": {"x": {"d": "The input batch: shape (batch, 784) — for example, 32 flattened 28x28 MNIST images, each 784 pixels.\n\nEvery layer in the network receives a batch like this and returns a new tensor of some other shape.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 4: activations and layers", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-4"}]}, "lin": {"d": "A Linear layer: owns two learned parameter tensors, a weight matrix W of shape (784, 256) and a bias vector b of shape (256,), computing x @ W + b.\n\nThis is the only layer here that owns parameters gradient descent updates; ReLU and softmax below compute a fixed function with nothing to learn.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: layers", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/03_layers"}]}, "relu": {"d": "ReLU (rectified linear unit): max(0, z), applied elementwise — a fixed nonlinearity with no learned parameters.\n\nIt zeroes negative values and passes positive ones through unchanged. Without a nonlinearity like this between linear layers, stacking any number of Linear layers would still only compute one big linear function.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: activation functions", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/03-deep-learning-core/04-activation-functions"}]}, "drop": {"d": "Dropout with p = 0.5: during training, randomly zeroes half the activations and scales the survivors by 1/(1-p) = 2, so the expected total stays the same.\n\nIts behaviour depends on the layer's mode — it drops nothing during evaluation. This is why calling the wrong mode changes a model's output without changing any weights."}, "eval": {"d": "eval(): switches every mode-dependent layer in the network to inference behaviour — dropout stops dropping, and (in other layers) batch normalisation stops updating its running statistics.\n\nAlaap calls this before every measurement, because scoring a model still in train() mode would score its dropout noise as if it were the model's real behaviour."}, "sm": {"d": "Softmax, used here as the output layer: turns raw scores into a probability distribution, subtracting the maximum value first for numerical stability before exponentiating.\n\nLike ReLU, it owns no learned parameters — it is a fixed function applied to whatever the last Linear layer produces."}}, "edges": {"x>lin": {"d": "The raw input batch is transformed by the Linear layer's weights and bias into a new (batch, 256) representation."}, "lin>relu": {"d": "The linear layer's output is passed through ReLU, zeroing any negative activations before they reach the next stage."}, "relu>drop": {"d": "The ReLU output is passed through dropout, which — only during training — randomly zeroes half of it."}, "drop>eval": {"d": "Dropout's behaviour is gated on the network's mode; calling eval() is what turns dropout off before Alaap scores a model."}, "relu>sm": {"d": "In this small network, the ReLU output also feeds directly into softmax as the final output layer, producing a probability distribution over classes."}}, "walk": ["x", "x>lin", "lin", "lin>relu", "relu", "relu>drop", "drop", "drop>eval", "eval", "relu>sm", "sm"]},
  "al:loop": {"nodes": {"zg": {"d": "zero_grad(): clears the .grad accumulated on every parameter from the previous step.\n\nGradients accumulate by default (each backward() call adds to .grad rather than replacing it), so skipping this step silently sums gradients across batches instead of using just the current one.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 8: optimisers and the training loop", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-8"}]}, "fw": {"d": "The forward pass: model(batch), running the input through every layer to produce a prediction.\n\nThis is also what builds the autograd tape for the backward pass that follows — every operation here is recorded as it happens."}, "ls": {"d": "The loss: criterion(out, y), comparing the model's prediction against the true labels with whatever loss function fits the task.\n\nIt reduces the whole batch's predictions to one scalar number, which is what backward() needs a gradient of."}, "bw": {"d": "backward(): walks the autograd tape from the loss back to every parameter, filling in each one's .grad via the chain rule.\n\nAfter this call, every parameter with requires_grad has a gradient telling the optimiser which direction reduces the loss — but no weight has changed yet."}, "st": {"d": "optimizer.step(): applies the update rule — SGD or Adam — to every parameter using its .grad.\n\nAdam keeps two running averages per parameter (first and second moment estimates), so together with the weights it holds three tensors' worth of state per parameter, against two for SGD with momentum (the weights plus one velocity term).", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: optimizers", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/07_optimizers"}, {"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: optimizers", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/03-deep-learning-core/06-optimizers"}]}, "sch": {"d": "Learning-rate scheduling and gradient clipping: decaying the learning rate over training, and clipping the global gradient norm to some maximum before it can destabilise the update.\n\nBoth run after the optimiser step and before the next batch, adjusting the conditions the next step will run under rather than this one's result."}, "ev": {"d": "eval() plus no_grad(): switches every mode-dependent layer to inference behaviour and stops autograd from building a tape at all.\n\nThis pairing runs once per epoch (not per batch) to validate and checkpoint the model — no_grad() saves the memory a tape would otherwise use, since no backward pass will follow."}}, "edges": {"zg>fw": {"d": "Gradients are cleared before the forward pass builds a new computation graph for this batch."}, "fw>ls": {"d": "The forward pass's prediction is compared against the true labels to produce a scalar loss."}, "ls>bw": {"d": "backward() is called on the scalar loss to fill in every parameter's gradient."}, "bw>st": {"d": "Once every parameter has a gradient, the optimiser applies its update rule to the weights."}, "st>sch": {"d": "After the step, the learning-rate schedule and gradient clipping are applied before the loop moves to the next batch."}, "sch>zg": {"d": "Once scheduling and clipping are applied, the loop returns to zero_grad() to start the next batch."}, "st>ev": {"d": "Once per epoch rather than once per batch, the loop also switches to evaluation mode to validate and checkpoint."}}, "walk": ["zg", "zg>fw", "fw", "fw>ls", "ls", "ls>bw", "bw", "bw>st", "st", "st>sch", "sch", "sch>zg", "st>ev", "ev"]},
  "al:loss": {"nodes": {"z": {"d": "Logits: the raw, unnormalised output of the last layer — any real numbers, before they are turned into probabilities.\n\nA classifier produces one logit per class; softmax and cross-entropy are what turn these into a probability and then a training signal."}, "p": {"d": "Softmax: p = exp(z - max) / sum(exp(z - max)), turning logits into a probability distribution that sums to 1.\n\nSubtracting the maximum logit before exponentiating avoids overflow without changing the result — exp of a very large number would otherwise overflow floating point."}, "ce": {"d": "Cross-entropy loss: -log(p[target]), the negative log probability the model assigned to the correct class.\n\nIt is large when the model is confidently wrong and near zero when it is confidently right, and it is what training actually minimises.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: losses", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/04_losses"}, {"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: loss functions", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/03-deep-learning-core/05-loss-functions"}, {"label": "Stage 5: losses, measuring how wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-5"}]}, "g": {"d": "The gradient of softmax and cross-entropy together, with respect to the logits: simply p - onehot(target).\n\nThis is large whenever the model is wrong and shrinks smoothly as it becomes correct — softmax and cross-entropy are paired specifically because their gradients combine this cleanly; the log in cross-entropy cancels the exponential in softmax."}, "mse": {"d": "The alternative: sigmoid activation paired with mean squared error loss, instead of softmax with cross-entropy.\n\nIt looks like a reasonable substitute for a classification loss, but the pairing behaves very differently under saturation, which is the point of this diagram."}, "g2": {"d": "The gradient of sigmoid plus MSE vanishes when the sigmoid saturates (its output is near 0 or 1), even if the prediction is completely wrong.\n\nBecause the sigmoid's own derivative approaches zero at the extremes, a confidently wrong prediction can produce almost no gradient — training stalls exactly when it most needs to correct itself."}}, "edges": {"z>p": {"d": "Logits are passed through softmax to become a probability distribution."}, "p>ce": {"d": "The predicted probability for the target class is what cross-entropy scores."}, "ce>g": {"d": "Differentiating cross-entropy through softmax gives the clean p - onehot gradient with respect to the logits."}, "mse>g2": {"d": "Differentiating MSE through a saturated sigmoid gives a gradient that shrinks toward zero, unlike the cross-entropy case."}}, "walk": ["z", "z>p", "p", "p>ce", "ce", "ce>g", "g", "mse", "mse>g2", "g2"]},
  "al:mdn": {"nodes": {"d": {"d": "\"a warm low voice\": free text describing a desired voice — the input to the whole mapping pipeline.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of description a person would type, not a structured set of parameters.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "e": {"d": "A sentence encoder (MiniLM): turns the free-text description into a fixed-length embedding.\n\nThis is the same idea as the embed diagram's lookup, but for a whole sentence rather than a single token — a pretrained model does the encoding rather than a table trained from scratch."}, "m": {"d": "A mixture density network with 10 Gaussians: outputs, for a given description embedding, a mixture of 10 Gaussian distributions over speaker-vector space — weights, means and spreads for each component.\n\nUnlike a regressor that predicts one point, this predicts an entire distribution, because many different real voices can plausibly match one description."}, "s": {"d": "Sampling a vector: drawing one concrete speaker vector from the predicted mixture distribution.\n\nSampling from a family of plausible voices, rather than always returning one fixed point, is what lets the same description produce different, equally valid voices on different calls."}, "loss": {"d": "Negative log-likelihood of the real speaker: the training loss, scoring how probable the ground-truth speaker vector is under the predicted mixture.\n\nThis is what forces the mixture's components to actually cover the range of real voices matching a description, rather than collapsing to one average."}, "reg": {"d": "A plain regressor: the alternative approach, predicting one single speaker vector directly instead of a distribution over them.\n\nTrained with squared error on a one-to-many mapping (many valid voices per description), a regressor learns to predict the average of all of them — a blurred, central voice that isn't a good match for any of them specifically."}}, "edges": {"d>e": {"d": "The free-text description is encoded into a fixed-length embedding."}, "e>m": {"d": "The description embedding conditions the mixture density network's output — a different mixture for every description."}, "m>s": {"d": "A concrete speaker vector is drawn by sampling from the predicted mixture, one of many plausible outcomes."}, "m>loss": {"d": "During training, the same mixture output is scored against the true speaker vector by negative log-likelihood."}}, "walk": ["d", "d>e", "e", "e>m", "m", "m>s", "s", "m>loss", "loss", "reg"]},
  "al:owned": {"nodes": {"d": {"d": "Description: free text describing a voice — what stage 14, tokens and embeddings, teaches you to turn into numbers.\n\nThis is the system's entry point, unchanged from stage 0's system-context picture; now you can explain the tokenizer and embedding table behind it.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 14: tokens and embeddings", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-14"}]}, "m": {"d": "The mapper (MDN): the mixture density network from the mdn diagram, trained using the loss mechanics from stage 5 and the training loop from stage 8.\n\nThree separate stages taught the pieces that combine into this one component: what the loss function does, how the training loop runs it, and what the mapper itself computes.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 5: losses, measuring how wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-5"}, {"label": "Stage 8: optimisers and the training loop", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-8"}, {"label": "Stage 17: description to voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-17"}]}, "id": {"d": "The identity store: the vector-space object from stage 13, 'is the space navigable?', holding a speaker vector plus a seed clip.\n\nStage 13 is what makes this store meaningful rather than a random vector — a poorly-conditioned space would make stored identities unreliable to retrieve or interpolate between.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 13: is the space navigable?", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-13"}]}, "r": {"d": "The frozen TTS renderer: the transformer and codec-decoder mechanism from stages 15 (attention and transformers) and 16 (how a TTS model actually works).\n\nIts weights are never updated; everything Alaap learns lives in the mapper and identity store around it, not in this component.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 15: attention and transformers", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-15"}, {"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}]}, "g": {"d": "The gates: drift, consistency, uniqueness and CER, resting on the speaker-embedding mechanism from stage 12 and the evaluation thinking from stage 18.\n\nStage 18's core lesson — that most evaluation is wrong until it's measured and calibrated, not assumed — is what justifies why these particular gates exist and where their thresholds come from.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 12: speaker identity, embeddings that recognise people", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-12"}, {"label": "Stage 18: evaluation, and why most of it is wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-18"}]}, "s": {"d": "The service, running over HTTP: what stage 19, 'making it run: inference, measured', teaches you to build and benchmark.\n\nThis is the same profile-quantise-prune-benchmark loop as the serve diagram, applied to the whole system rather than one model.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 19: making it run, inference measured", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-19"}]}, "a": {"d": "Audio, per line: produced using the sampling and resampling mechanics of stage 1, the source-filter measurements of stage 2, and the STFT mechanics of stage 11.\n\nEvery number that describes this audio — its sample rate, its spectral content, its identity axes — traces back to one of these three stages.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 1: sound as data, sampling and Nyquist", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-1"}, {"label": "Stage 2: source and filter, what describes a voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-2"}, {"label": "Stage 11: the rest of sound, frequency and how estimators lie", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-11"}]}}, "edges": {"d>m": {"d": "A description is mapped to a speaker-vector distribution by the trained MDN mapper."}, "m>id": {"d": "A sampled vector, plus a seed clip, is written into the identity store, becoming a stored, reusable identity."}, "id>r": {"d": "A stored identity is what the frozen renderer consumes to produce speech in that voice."}, "r>g": {"d": "Everything the renderer produces passes through the gates before being accepted."}, "g>a": {"d": "Once gated, audio is produced per line of a script."}, "r>s": {"d": "The renderer is also what the HTTP service wraps and serves at scale."}}, "walk": ["d", "d>m", "m", "m>id", "id", "id>r", "r", "r>g", "g", "g>a", "a", "r>s", "s"]},
  "al:sampling": {"nodes": {"wave": {"d": "Air pressure varying continuously over time — the physical signal before anything is measured.\n\nSound is a continuous wave; a computer can only store discrete numbers, so every digital audio pipeline starts by choosing how often to read this wave.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 1: sampling and Nyquist", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-1"}]}, "s24": {"d": "The training corpus recorded at 24,000 samples per second — 24 readings of air pressure every millisecond.\n\nThis is Alaap's native measurement rate, chosen to comfortably capture adult speech content while keeping file sizes down. It is not the rate every downstream model wants, which is why resampling appears in this diagram.", "links": [{"label": "Session: sampling and Nyquist, worked through", "url": "index.html#/s/3/3"}]}, "ny": {"d": "The Nyquist frequency: half the sample rate, the highest frequency a given sample rate can represent without ambiguity.\n\nAt 24 kHz that ceiling is 12 kHz. Any real content above it cannot be told apart from a lower frequency once sampled — it has to be removed before resampling down, or it comes back as aliasing.", "links": [{"label": "Reading: why a sample rate represents frequencies up to half itself", "url": "https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/mdft/Sampling_Theorem.html"}]}, "lp": {"d": "A low-pass filter that removes frequency content above the new, lower Nyquist limit, applied before samples are actually dropped.\n\n'Keep 2 of every 3' describes downsampling from 24 kHz to 16 kHz (a 3:2 ratio) after filtering — the filter runs first so nothing above 8 kHz survives to be misrepresented.", "links": [{"label": "Reading: what changing sample rate costs, and why filter first", "url": "https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/resample/"}]}, "s16": {"d": "The corpus resampled to 16 kHz for WavLM, a speech model trained at that rate.\n\nContent up to 8 kHz survives because the low-pass filter already discarded anything higher. Feeding 24 kHz audio straight into a 16 kHz-trained model without this filtered resample would corrupt the input."}, "alias": {"d": "What happens if the low-pass filter is skipped before downsampling: a frequency above the new Nyquist limit does not disappear, it reappears at the wrong, lower frequency.\n\nThe example given is a real 10 kHz component folding down to a false 6 kHz component once sampled at 16 kHz — indistinguishable from genuine 6 kHz content, and impossible to undo afterward."}}, "edges": {"wave>s24": {"d": "The continuous pressure wave is read at fixed intervals, 24,000 times a second, turning it into the digital corpus."}, "s24>lp": {"d": "The 24 kHz signal is fed into the low-pass filter before any samples are discarded, so the filter sees the full original content."}, "lp>s16": {"d": "The filtered signal is then downsampled, keeping 2 of every 3 samples to land at 16 kHz."}, "s24>ny": {"d": "The 24 kHz rate sets the Nyquist ceiling at 12 kHz — a fact about the corpus, not a further processing step."}, "lp>alias": {"d": "Skipping the filter and downsampling directly is what produces aliasing; this edge is the road not taken."}}, "walk": ["wave", "wave>s24", "s24", "s24>ny", "ny", "s24>lp", "lp", "lp>s16", "s16", "lp>alias", "alias"]},
  "al:serve": {"nodes": {"p": {"d": "Profiling: measuring FLOPs, memory use, and where wall-clock time actually goes in a model, before trying to speed anything up.\n\nWithout this step, optimisation effort routinely goes into the wrong place — a component that looks slow is not the same as the component that is actually the bottleneck.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: profiling", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/14_profiling"}]}, "q": {"d": "Quantisation: converting weights (and sometimes activations) from float32 to a lower-precision format like int8, roughly a 4x reduction in size.\n\nSmaller weights mean less memory bandwidth spent moving them, which is often the real bottleneck in inference, not raw compute.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: quantization", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/15_quantization"}]}, "c": {"d": "Pruning and distillation: removing weights that contribute little (pruning) or training a smaller model to mimic a larger one's outputs (distillation) — both reduce the number of weights.\n\nUnlike quantisation, this changes the model's parameter count itself, not just how each parameter is stored.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: compression", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/16_compression"}]}, "a": {"d": "Fusing and vectorising: combining multiple operations into one kernel and using SIMD/vector instructions, both aimed at reducing the number of separate memory trips per operation.\n\nEven a numerically identical computation can run much faster if it touches memory fewer times.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: acceleration", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/17_acceleration"}]}, "kv": {"d": "KV (key-value) caching: reusing the keys and values already computed for previous tokens in an autoregressive model, instead of recomputing attention over the whole sequence at every new token.\n\nThis is what makes token-by-token generation practical — without it, generating the nth token would redo all the attention work for the first n-1 tokens.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: memoization", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/18_memoization"}, {"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: KV cache and flash attention", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/07-transformers-deep-dive/12-kv-cache-flash-attention"}]}, "b": {"d": "Benchmarking: measuring p95 latency and running it inside a confidence interval, not just an average.\n\nAn average latency can look fine while a meaningful fraction of requests are unacceptably slow; p95 is what a production SLA is usually built around.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: benchmarking", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/19_benchmarking"}]}, "alaap": {"d": "Alaap's own stages S6 and S7 — internal platform milestones, a different numbering from this self-study's stages 0 to 21 — are blocked on exactly this: a service that renders over HTTP, with p95 latency and cost per minute measured under real load.\n\nThis whole profile-quantise-prune-benchmark loop is what stage 19, 'making it run: inference, measured,' has you build and run for real.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 19: making it run, inference measured", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-19"}]}}, "edges": {"p>q": {"d": "Profiling identifies where to spend optimisation effort; some of it goes toward precision, via quantisation."}, "q>a": {"d": "Quantised weights still benefit from fusing and vectorising the operations that use them."}, "p>c": {"d": "Profiling also identifies where to spend effort reducing weight count, via pruning and distillation."}, "c>kv": {"d": "Once a model is smaller from pruning or distillation, KV caching further cuts the per-token cost of generation."}, "a>b": {"d": "Fused, vectorised, quantised inference is what actually gets measured next."}, "kv>b": {"d": "The benefit of KV caching is verified the same way, by benchmarking, not by assumption."}, "b>alaap": {"d": "The measured, benchmarked pipeline is exactly what Alaap's own platform milestones, S6 and S7, are waiting on before they can ship."}}, "walk": ["p", "p>q", "q", "p>c", "c", "c>kv", "kv", "q>a", "a", "a>b", "kv>b", "b", "b>alaap", "alaap"]},
  "al:sourcefilter": {"nodes": {"folds": {"d": "The vocal folds (vocal cords): two flaps of tissue in the larynx that vibrate open and close, producing a buzz at the fundamental frequency.\n\nThis buzz is the sound source in the source-filter model of speech — it changes when someone raises or lowers their pitch, and is largely independent of what words are being said.", "links": [{"label": "Session: the source-filter model", "url": "index.html#/s/5/3"}]}, "tract": {"d": "The vocal tract: the tube of air from the vocal folds to the lips (throat, mouth, nasal cavity), which resonates at frequencies set by its shape.\n\nIt filters the buzz from the vocal folds, boosting some frequencies (formants) and damping others. Moving the tongue, jaw and lips reshapes it, turning a buzz into distinguishable vowels and consonants."}, "speech": {"d": "The audible result of the vocal-fold buzz passed through the vocal-tract filter: a source shaped by a filter.\n\nEvery acoustic measurement Alaap takes from a recording is trying to recover one side or the other of this split — which part is 'who is speaking' (the source) and which part is 'what shape are they making' (the filter).", "links": [{"label": "Stage 2: source and filter, what describes a voice", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-2"}]}, "f0": {"d": "f0_mean: the average fundamental frequency of the vocal-fold buzz across an utterance, in Hz — a source measurement.\n\nIt is the single strongest and cleanest identity axis Alaap measures (within/between-speaker ratio 0.08), because it depends almost entirely on vocal-fold physiology, not on what is being said."}, "vtl": {"d": "vtl_cm and the formant frequencies: estimates of vocal-tract length and its resonances — filter measurements.\n\nA longer tract produces lower formants, which is why vtl_cm reads as an identity axis (it was unusable noise until its estimator was fixed at stage 17). Formants also shift with the vowel being spoken, mixing identity with content unless measured carefully.", "links": [{"label": "Reading: formants and resonant frequency, mapped to f0, VTL and formant measurements", "url": "https://www.phon.ox.ac.uk/source_filter_model"}]}, "tilt": {"d": "spectral_tilt: the overall slope of the spectral envelope, meant to capture a source property (breathiness, vocal effort) but measured from the mixed speech signal.\n\nIt straddles the source-filter split: in practice it correlates strongly with f0 (r = +0.918) rather than isolating a separate axis — a feature intended to measure one thing that leaks the other."}}, "edges": {"folds>tract": {"d": "The vocal folds' periodic buzz is the input signal that excites the vocal tract's resonances — the acoustic term for driving a resonant system with a source."}, "tract>speech": {"d": "The tract's filtering of the source buzz produces the audible speech waveform; this is where source and filter combine into one signal."}, "speech>f0": {"d": "f0_mean is extracted from the periodicity of the combined signal, recovering the source's pitch even though only the mixed waveform was recorded."}, "speech>vtl": {"d": "vtl_cm and the formants are extracted from the spectral envelope of the combined signal, recovering filter properties from the same mixed waveform."}, "speech>tilt": {"d": "spectral_tilt is measured the same way, from the mixed signal — exactly why it ends up correlated with the source measurement (f0) instead of staying a clean filter measurement."}}, "walk": ["folds", "folds>tract", "tract", "tract>speech", "speech", "speech>f0", "f0", "speech>vtl", "vtl", "speech>tilt", "tilt"]},
  "al:stft": {"nodes": {"w": {"d": "The raw audio samples at 24 kHz — the same corpus rate as the sampling diagram.\n\nThe STFT (short-time Fourier transform) is what turns this 1-D sequence of samples into a 2-D time-frequency representation.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 11: the rest of sound, frequency and how estimators lie", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-11"}]}, "win": {"d": "n_fft = 2048: the analysis window length — 2048 samples at 24 kHz is about 85 milliseconds — shaped by a Hann window to reduce spectral leakage at its edges.\n\nA longer window gives finer frequency resolution but blurs together events that happen close together in time, the core STFT trade-off."}, "fft": {"d": "The FFT (fast Fourier transform): computed on each windowed segment, producing 1025 frequency bins (n_fft/2 + 1 for a real-valued signal), each 11.7 Hz wide (24000 / 2048).\n\nThis is the step that actually converts a segment of samples from the time domain into the frequency domain."}, "spec": {"d": "The spectrogram: the FFT output for every window position, stacked into a time-by-frequency grid.\n\nThis is the 2-D representation most audio models and measurements, including spectral_tilt, are computed from, rather than from raw samples directly."}, "hop": {"d": "hop = 512 samples: how far the window slides between successive FFTs, about 21 milliseconds at 24 kHz.\n\nA hop smaller than the window length means windows overlap; a smaller hop gives finer time resolution at the cost of more windows, and more compute, per second of audio.", "links": [{"label": "scipy.signal.resample docs", "url": "https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.resample.html"}]}, "trade": {"d": "The time/frequency trade-off: a longer window gives sharper frequency resolution (narrower Hz bins) but blurrier time resolution, and vice versa — a property of the Fourier transform itself, not a limitation of any particular implementation.\n\nBoth numbers, n_fft and hop, appear inside spectral_tilt's computation, which is why getting them right matters for that specific measurement."}}, "edges": {"w>win": {"d": "The raw samples are cut into overlapping windows of length n_fft before each one is transformed."}, "win>fft": {"d": "Each windowed segment of samples is transformed by the FFT into frequency-bin magnitudes."}, "fft>spec": {"d": "The FFT output for every window position is stacked to build the full spectrogram."}, "hop>win": {"d": "The hop length determines how far each successive window is shifted along the signal."}, "spec>trade": {"d": "The resulting spectrogram's resolution in both axes is governed by the time/frequency trade-off set by window length and hop."}}, "walk": ["w", "w>win", "win", "hop", "hop>win", "win>fft", "fft", "fft>spec", "spec", "spec>trade", "trade"]},
  "al:tensor": {"nodes": {"buf": {"d": "A flat buffer: a single contiguous block of float32 numbers in memory, with no notion of rows or columns.\n\nEvery tensor is ultimately this — a flat array — plus metadata that says how to interpret it. Nothing about shape is stored in the data itself.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: Tensor", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/01_tensor"}, {"label": "Stage 3: tensors, built by hand", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-3"}]}, "shape": {"d": "Shape and strides: shape says how many elements lie along each dimension (32, 80); strides say how many elements to skip in the flat buffer to move one step along each dimension ((80, 1) here).\n\nTogether they let the same flat buffer be read as a 2-D array without copying it. Reading position (i, j) means indexing i*80 + j*1 into the buffer."}, "t": {"d": "Transpose: swapping which stride goes with which dimension, without touching the underlying buffer.\n\nBecause the data itself never moves, transpose is free — an O(1) metadata change, not an O(n) copy. This is the payoff of separating 'buffer' from 'shape + strides'."}, "bc": {"d": "Broadcasting: an operation between two tensors of different shapes where the smaller one is treated as if repeated to match the larger one's shape, without actually copying it.\n\n(32, 80) + (80,) works because the length-80 vector is conceptually repeated across all 32 rows; only one copy of it exists in memory.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: vectors and matrices operations (broadcasting)", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/01-math-foundations/02-vectors-matrices-operations"}]}, "mm": {"d": "Matrix multiplication: (32, 80) @ (80, 256) — every row of the first matrix is combined with every column of the second.\n\nThe inner dimensions (80 and 80) must match; this is the shape rule that catches most tensor bugs before anything even runs."}, "out": {"d": "The result of the broadcast add: shape (32, 80), the same shape as the larger input.\n\nNo second copy of the (80,) vector was ever made — broadcasting computes the sum on the fly, row by row."}, "out2": {"d": "The result of the matmul: shape (32, 256) — the outer dimensions of the two inputs, with the shared inner dimension (80) consumed.\n\nGetting this shape right depends entirely on the inner dimensions matching, which is why matmul shape errors are the most common tensor bug."}}, "edges": {"buf>shape": {"d": "The flat buffer is given a shape and strides, turning an undifferentiated block of numbers into something that can be indexed as a 2-D array."}, "shape>t": {"d": "Transpose reads the same buffer through a different shape/stride pairing — a view, not a copy."}, "shape>bc": {"d": "The (32, 80) tensor is one operand of a broadcast add against a (80,) vector."}, "bc>out": {"d": "The broadcast add produces a (32, 80) result without materializing a second copy of the smaller operand."}, "shape>mm": {"d": "The (32, 80) tensor is also fed into a matmul against a (80, 256) weight matrix."}, "mm>out2": {"d": "The matmul produces a (32, 256) result; the shared inner dimension (80) must match on both inputs or this step fails."}}, "walk": ["buf", "buf>shape", "shape", "shape>t", "t", "shape>bc", "bc", "bc>out", "out", "shape>mm", "mm", "mm>out2", "out2"]},
  "al:torchmap": {"nodes": {"a1": {"d": "Tensor, from trentorch.core.tensor — the hand-built buffer-plus-shape-and-strides type from stage 3.\n\nEvery operation implemented on it (indexing, transpose, broadcasting, matmul) has a direct counterpart in torch.Tensor.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: tensor", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/01_tensor"}]}, "b1": {"d": "torch.Tensor: PyTorch's real tensor type, adding .to(device) (move between CPU and GPU) and configurable dtype (float32, float16, and so on) on top of the same buffer-plus-shape-and-strides mechanism.\n\nThe underlying idea — a flat buffer read through shape and strides — is identical; devices and dtypes are what production adds."}, "a2": {"d": "Function and backward, from trentorch.core.autograd — the hand-built tape-recording mechanism from stage 7.\n\nEach operation with a registered backward rule is the same shape as a PyTorch autograd Function.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: autograd", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/06_autograd"}]}, "b2": {"d": "PyTorch's autograd, no_grad() and detach(): the same tape mechanism, with no_grad() stopping recording (as in stage 7) and detach() cutting a tensor loose from the graph it was built in.\n\nIt is the same tape you built, running at production scale."}, "a3": {"d": "Linear and Dropout, from trentorch.core.layers — the hand-built layer types from stage 4, one owning parameters and one behaving differently by mode.\n\nThese map directly onto PyTorch's nn.Module-based layers.", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: layers", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/03_layers"}]}, "b3": {"d": "nn.Module and eval(): PyTorch's base class for any layer or model, distinguishing learned parameters from buffers (non-learned state like batch-norm running statistics), with eval() as the same mode switch from stage 4.\n\nThe parameter/buffer split is the production version of 'owns weights vs. owns nothing' from your own Linear and Dropout.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: intro to PyTorch", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/03-deep-learning-core/11-intro-to-pytorch"}]}, "a4": {"d": "Trainer and DataLoader, from trentorch.core.training — the hand-built training loop and batch-loading logic from stages 6 and 8.\n\nThese map onto torch.optim (optimisers) and torch.utils.data (dataset and loader utilities).", "links": [{"label": "TrenTorch module: training", "url": "https://github.com/TrenTorch/TrenTorch/tree/main/data/src/08_training"}]}, "b4": {"d": "PyTorch's optim and utils.data: the same five-line training loop and shuffle-batch-collate pipeline built by hand, packaged as a library.\n\nNothing about the mechanism changes — only that it is now implemented in C++ and tuned for scale."}}, "edges": {"a1>b1": {"d": "The TrenTorch tensor type and torch.Tensor implement the same buffer-plus-shape-and-strides mechanism; the differences are devices, dtypes and scale, not the underlying idea."}, "a2>b2": {"d": "The TrenTorch autograd tape and PyTorch's autograd record and replay the same kind of graph; no_grad() and detach() are production refinements of the same idea."}, "a3>b3": {"d": "TrenTorch's Linear and Dropout and PyTorch's nn.Module-based layers draw the same line between owning parameters and owning nothing."}, "a4>b4": {"d": "The hand-written training loop and PyTorch's optim/utils.data run the identical five-step mechanism, just implemented for production."}}, "walk": ["a1", "a1>b1", "b1", "a2", "a2>b2", "b2", "a3", "a3>b3", "b3", "a4", "a4>b4", "b4"]},
  "al:ttslm": {"nodes": {"txt": {"d": "Text tokens: what to say, tokenized the same way as any language model's input.\n\nThis is the content half of the generation, independent of who is speaking it.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 16: how a TTS model actually works", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-16"}]}, "spk": {"d": "The speaker vector: who says it — a fixed-length embedding (from the xvector diagram's ECAPA-TDNN, or the mdn diagram's mapper) identifying a voice.\n\nIt conditions two separate stages of generation: the transformer that predicts content, and the codec decoder that renders it into audio."}, "lm": {"d": "A transformer: predicts codec tokens autoregressively, conditioned on both the text tokens and the speaker vector.\n\nThis is the same attention mechanism from the attention diagram, just predicting discrete audio-codec ids instead of the next word."}, "tok": {"d": "Content tokens: discrete audio-codec ids at low bitrate, here using an FSQ (finite scalar quantisation) codebook with levels [8, 8, 8, 5, 5] — what to say, in the codec's own vocabulary.\n\nThese encode linguistic content only; the speaker vector supplies identity separately at the decoding stage."}, "dec": {"d": "The codec decoder: takes the predicted content tokens together with the speaker identity and renders them into a waveform.\n\nBecause content tokens carry no identity of their own, the same generated tokens can be decoded through any speaker vector to produce the same words in a different voice.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: neural audio codecs", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/13-neural-audio-codecs"}]}, "wav": {"d": "The output waveform, at 44.1 kHz.\n\nThis is the final audio a listener hears — everything before this node is discrete tokens and vectors, not sound."}}, "edges": {"txt>lm": {"d": "Text tokens condition the transformer's prediction of what content comes next."}, "spk>lm": {"d": "The speaker vector also conditions the transformer, alongside the text tokens, before content-token generation."}, "lm>tok": {"d": "The transformer autoregressively predicts one content token at a time."}, "tok>dec": {"d": "The generated content tokens are the codec decoder's first input."}, "spk>dec": {"d": "The speaker vector is applied a second time, at decoding, supplying the identity the content tokens don't carry."}, "dec>wav": {"d": "The codec decoder renders tokens plus identity into the final audio waveform."}}, "walk": ["txt", "spk", "txt>lm", "spk>lm", "lm", "lm>tok", "tok", "tok>dec", "spk>dec", "dec", "dec>wav", "wav"]},
  "al:vendi": {"nodes": {"v": {"d": "n voices: a set of speaker vectors whose diversity is being measured — for example, forty minted voices.\n\nThe question Vendi score answers is not 'how many voices are there' (that's just n) but 'how many distinct voices are there.'", "links": [{"label": "Stage 18: evaluation, and why most of it is wrong", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-18"}]}, "k": {"d": "The similarity matrix: an (n, n) matrix of pairwise cosine similarities between every pair of voices.\n\nThis is the input to Vendi score's eigenvalue computation — it captures how alike or different every pair of voices is, not just each voice on its own."}, "eig": {"d": "Eigenvalues of the similarity matrix, normalised to sum to 1: this turns the matrix into something that behaves like a probability distribution over 'directions' of variation among the voices.\n\nA set of near-identical voices concentrates almost all its weight on one eigenvalue; a set of very different voices spreads weight more evenly."}, "ent": {"d": "exp(entropy) of the normalised eigenvalues: the Vendi score, interpreted as 'effective number of distinct voices.'\n\nForty voices that are really twenty distinct ones score close to twenty, not forty — this is the whole point of the metric.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: audio evaluation metrics", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/17-audio-evaluation-metrics"}]}, "trap": {"d": "The trap: a normalised Vendi score (dividing by n) is a fraction, not a count, and the same fraction means a different number of effective voices depending on how large n is.\n\nComparing normalised scores across sets of different sizes without converting back to an actual count is a common way this metric gets misread."}}, "edges": {"v>k": {"d": "Every pair of voices is compared by cosine similarity to build the similarity matrix."}, "k>eig": {"d": "The similarity matrix is decomposed into eigenvalues, its spectral structure."}, "eig>ent": {"d": "The normalised eigenvalues' entropy, exponentiated, gives the effective count of distinct voices."}, "ent>trap": {"d": "Reading a normalised version of this score, divided by n, as if it were the count itself, is the trap the caption warns about."}}, "walk": ["v", "v>k", "k", "k>eig", "eig", "eig>ent", "ent", "ent>trap", "trap"]},
  "al:xvector": {"nodes": {"utt": {"d": "An utterance from one of thousands of speakers in a training corpus — the raw input to speaker-embedding training.\n\nThe training objective (tell speakers apart) is what shapes everything the resulting embedding does and doesn't capture.", "links": [{"label": "Stage 12: speaker identity, embeddings that recognise people", "url": "ALAAP.html#/plan/stage-12"}]}, "enc": {"d": "ECAPA-TDNN: the convolution-and-pooling architecture from the conv1d diagram, trained here as a speaker classifier.\n\nIts job during training is to output, for each utterance, a prediction of which of the thousands of training speakers it came from.", "links": [{"label": "AI Engineering from Scratch: speaker recognition and verification", "url": "https://github.com/rohitg00/ai-engineering-from-scratch/tree/main/phases/06-speech-and-audio/06-speaker-recognition-verification"}]}, "emb": {"d": "The penultimate layer's output: a fixed-length vector, taken from the layer just before the classification head.\n\nThis is the actual speaker embedding used at inference time — not the classifier's output, which only exists during training and is discarded afterward."}, "cls": {"d": "The speaker classifier: the final layer that maps the embedding to one of the thousands of training-speaker classes.\n\nIt exists purely to create a training signal; it is thrown away once training finishes, since production use only needs the embedding it forces the network to produce."}, "cos": {"d": "Cosine similarity between two embeddings: measures how aligned two speaker vectors are, independent of their magnitude, answering 'same speaker or different?'\n\nThis is the standard operation for comparing any two speaker embeddings, whether for verification or for Alaap's identity gates."}, "thr": {"d": "In-domain calibration: thresholds set from measurements on Alaap's own corpus — 0.70 for 'same speaker' (C_same) and 0.20 for 'different speaker' (C_diff) — rather than borrowed from a paper.\n\nA threshold calibrated on someone else's data and someone else's encoder does not necessarily transfer; these numbers were measured, not assumed."}}, "edges": {"utt>enc": {"d": "The utterance is the encoder's input, regardless of whether it's the training phase or later inference."}, "enc>emb": {"d": "The encoder's penultimate-layer output is the fixed-length embedding used everywhere downstream."}, "emb>cls": {"d": "During training only, the embedding also feeds a classifier that forces the network to make embeddings separable by speaker; this path doesn't exist at inference time."}, "emb>cos": {"d": "At inference time, two embeddings are compared directly by cosine similarity — the classifier is never involved."}, "cos>thr": {"d": "The raw cosine similarity is compared against calibrated thresholds to decide same-speaker or different-speaker."}}, "walk": ["utt", "utt>enc", "enc", "enc>emb", "emb", "emb>cls", "cls", "emb>cos", "cos", "cos>thr", "thr"]}
});
